WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.520
- Well, welcome everyone.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.680
It's really great to see so
many of you in the Zoom room

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.200
and I wanna thank you on behalf

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.180
of the department of
African-American studies

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.180
for making the time to
think and to critique

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:26.510
and to plan with us in what
has been an extraordinary set

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.660
of seminars this past semester

00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:34.770
that we've devoted to
really thinking about

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.180
and learning about new modes of activism

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.990
to address the crises
that are shaping our time.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.260
We thought about elections
and voting rights

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.853
which are still haunting and
aching at us as we speak.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.540
We've been thinking about healthcare

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.010
and as a justice movement especially

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.840
for formerly incarcerated people.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.020
We've been thinking about
the role of art and artists

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.920
in protests and social justice movements,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.310
and today and in the
next and final session

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:12.810
that we'll be hosting this semester,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.800
we're gonna be thinking
about the histories,

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.830
the cultural logics, the
institutional practices and agents

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.170
that manage the carceral state,

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.550
and to think about how we dismantle it

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.780
or to use a phrase that I was reminded

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.560
of teaching James Baldwins great novel

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.400
about this very crisis, "If
Beale Street Could Talk",

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.730
how do we take apart, the jaws

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:38.070
of this democratic hell called the prison,

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.790
called the police state?

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.840
So, that's our discussion today.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.500
My colleague Crystal Feimster

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.730
will introduce our panelists shortly.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.240
I just want to point to
the next conversation

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.440
we'll be having about this topic

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.770
on December 10th after
Thanksgiving break at 11:45.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.230
Our colleague Elizabeth Hinton,

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.460
wonderful brilliant
historian of the history

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.260
of mass incarceration
will be in conversation

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.600
with Tracey Meares, our colleague
in ally in the law school

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.080
and they'll be talking about the work

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.410
that the Justice Collaboratory

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:16.010
that's housed in the law school.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.010
They'll explain that group's work

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.310
and we can open up how we
might partner with them

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.780
in the great work that they're doing.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.480
I wanna give thanks, excuse me, here to,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.430
you know, nothing can
happen without collaboration

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.400
in the age of Zoom especially,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.400
and I wanna thank our
co-sponsor for these seminars,

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:38.290
the Center for the Study
of Race, Indigeneity

