00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:27.730
And on that note, I wanna
say it is both an honor

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:31.420
and a privilege to be able to present

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.510
and introduce two of my colleagues

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.030
in African-American studies.

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.527
I'll start with Phillip Atiba Goff,

00:05:39.527 --> 00:05:42.111
I hope I said your middle name right.

00:05:42.111 --> 00:05:46.160
(indistinct) names, but
I'mma say Atiba again

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.740
and then you can correct me later, okay.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.160
As a professor of African-American studies

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:55.940
and psychology at Yale
University, yay, welcome,

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:58.150
and the co-founder and CEO

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.660
of the Center for Policing Equity,

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.360
a research and action think
tank on race and policing.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.030
He is a leading scholar in
the science of racial bias

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:10.830
and has pioneered scientific experiments

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:14.200
that expose how our minds
learn to associate blackness

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:19.200
and crime implicitly often
with deadly consequences.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:23.300
Dr. Goff has won two American
Psychological Association

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:24.920
early career awards,

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:28.760
the Association of Psychological
Science Rising Star Award

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:30.490
and the National Organization

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:35.450
of Black Law Enforcement
Executives Lloyd G Sealy Award

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.240
among many others.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.240
He regularly appears on Cable news,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.830
provides congressional testimony

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.760
and with a panelist for the
president Obama task force

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.323
on 21st century policing.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:52.460
I'd like to turn to his
interlocutor today too

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.870
will be framing and
moderating our discussion,

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:00.870
and that is Phil McHarris,
who is a PhD candidate

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:05.470
in the joint program of
African-American studies

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:08.897
in sociology at Yale University, hi.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.810
Phil's main area of research
include race and ethnicity,

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:17.510
housing, policing, and inequality.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:20.990
His current research focuses
on the experience of residents

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:23.690
of a high rise public housing development

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:25.130
in Brooklyn, New York

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:28.900
as they navigate concerns
surrounding safety, policing,

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:32.270
building conditions and cycles of poverty.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:36.530
In another line of his research,
Philip examined the causes

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:40.400
and consequences of the large
scale expansion of policing

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.410
in the United States since 1965

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.860
and the strategies that communities employ

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:49.990
to challenge police expansion
and police violence.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:53.240
Philip draws on qualitative
and quantitative methods

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.910
throughout his research.

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:58.530
In addition to his scholarly
work, Philip has written

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:00.830
for outlets that include
the New York Times,

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:03.390
Washington Post and Slate.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:08.390
His commentary has been featured
on HBO, CNN, Time and PBS.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.270
Philip is a recipient

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:13.210
of the Ford Foundation
pre-doctoral Fellowship

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:16.700
and the NSF Graduate Research Fellowship.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:21.700
Welcome and I'm gonna turn it
over to Phil M at this point.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.980
- Well, I have to keep track
of the Phil M and Phil G,

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.903
but I'm thanking you for that actually.

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:31.140
Yeah, hello everyone,
it's a pleasure to be here

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:32.160
with you all today

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:35.611
and I'm eager to have this
discussion around policing.

00:08:35.611 --> 00:08:38.500
I wanna offer a few points to
ground our conversation today.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.780
But first I'm gonna lift up
all those who have gotten us

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.870
to a place where we can have conversations

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:45.247
around radically
re-imagining public safety

00:08:45.247 --> 00:08:47.093
and ways for caring for one another.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:49.470
To that end, I wanna lift up

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:52.120
the Movement for Black Lives
organization in Minneapolis,

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.620
namely Reclaim the Block,
Black Visions Collective

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:57.480
and MPD 150 and those in Ferguson

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.810
that on August 9th, 2014
decided enough was enough,

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:02.780
played a large role in
launching this iteration

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:04.530
of the long black freedom struggle.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.890
We find ourselves at a
critical juncture in history

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.750
as it relates to policing
and public safety.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.220
There's been a resurgence of
the Movement for Black Lives.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.540
Over the last five years
organizers have been building

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.790
and developing politically
to bring us to a moment

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.673
where we can see and push for
much different set of demands.

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:25.540
Specifically the nation
has seen the injection

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:29.150
of abolitionists language and
demands to popular discourse

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:31.180
in ways that is unprecedented.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.770
Abolitionists and those
calling for dramatic shrinking

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:35.440
and the scope and power of policing

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:36.930
have grown increasingly skeptical

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:38.857
of popular seemingly progressive reforms

00:09:38.857 --> 00:09:40.680
that have been raised a solution

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.303
in recent years and decades.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:46.700
Procedural justice, bias
training, community policing,

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:49.300
diversity efforts, use
of force guidelines,

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.520
civilian review boards, body cameras,

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.670
focused deterrents and training centered

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:56.540
on police legitimacy have all

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:58.690
been fundamentally called into question

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:01.740
as many municipalities
have enacted these reforms.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.760
While the impacts of the
various strategies vary

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.110
from negligible to having some
impact on particular metrics,

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:08.990
they have proven ineffective
in stopping cycles

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:10.093
of police violence.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:14.420
Abolitionists such as Angela
Davis, Ruth Wilson Gilmore,

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.050
Mariame Kaba and organizers
from Critical Resistance

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.840
and so many others have
offered black feminist visions

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:22.580
of the world that sees the
utter and complete dismantling

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:26.420
of systems of imprisonment,
policing and surveillance

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.040
and the creation of new ways of relating

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:30.670
and caring for one another,
leading to transformation

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:32.930
and ensuring communities
have their basic needs met

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:35.790
and the resources to thrive,
which has been so critical

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:38.270
to bringing us to the present
moment we find ourselves in

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:41.710
where we can have very different
kinds of conversations.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:43.700
To be sure the persistent policing

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.280
of black and marginalized communities

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:46.870
is tied up in larger systems of racial

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:50.710
and class inequalities essentially
racial capitalism itself.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.580
Those who have presented
visions of abolition

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:54.350
and reallocation have argued

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:56.440
for the start of the large scale reduction

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:58.210
and dismantling of punitive systems

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:01.680
in favor of the creation
of just, democratic

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.110
and non-coercive as Ruth
Wilson Gilmore states,

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:06.310
life affirming institutions.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:08.910
Specifically, recent calls for defunding

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.870
which anti-violence organizer Mariame Kaba

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:12.000
has called a moderate demand

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.900
within a larger abolitionist framework

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.430
have called for the shift
of resources from policing

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:18.980
to the resources that have been proven

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:22.290
to increase safety in communities
including quality housing,

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:25.300
dignifying employment,
health care, resource schools

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:26.600
as well as alternative emergency

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:28.113
and crisis response systems.

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:31.440
Even more organizers have
called for a re-imagining

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.040
of the ways we address conflict, harm

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:34.480
and violence centered on frameworks

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:35.940
such as transformative justice

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:37.460
rather than punitive approaches

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:40.310
that have been so fine tuned
in America's carceral state.

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:42.850
And to ground us, I wanna center

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:45.010
in this conversation, all
those who are no longer with us

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:47.030
as a result of police violence,

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:49.660
those who have survived it
and all those, including

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:50.947
those on the front lines of protest

00:11:50.947 --> 00:11:54.200
and organizing efforts that
continue to experience it

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.330
in order to bring about a
radically different society

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:59.900
and approach to public safety
than the one we see today.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:01.970
And lastly, I also wanna hold all those

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:03.890
who are trapped in the
cages of incarceration

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:07.130
in the United States who
likely needed resources,

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:10.270
safe housing, mental health
treatment, care, support,

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:13.830
food, quality education,
meaningful employment

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.700
or arrested in front of, into
America's jails and prisons

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:18.740
as well as those who have
been jailed and incarcerated

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.650
throughout the country for protesting

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:22.750
and challenging state violence.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.410
My hope is that we can route
this conversation today

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:27.060
in the long black freedom struggle

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:30.030
and the fight to end
cycles of premature death

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:32.300
and ensuring communities have
the resources and conditions

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:33.500
to live life more fully.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.850
So, let's turn to the conversation.

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:39.400
Again, you know, I hope this conversation

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:41.360
can be a generative
and productive dialogue

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:45.130
that can bring us to having
a more sense of clarity

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:46.440
on all the words and concepts

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:47.710
we've heard over the past few months

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:51.270
from abolition to reform
to the funding and more.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.140
And, you know, I probably
have more questions

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:54.180
than we have time for today,

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.230
but, you know, I imagine that
as mentioned, Professor Goff

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.070
and I will engage in conversation

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:03.850
for around 50 minutes or so
before we include questions.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.560
So, let's start Professor Goff,

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:08.750
you know, you've been involved
in work around policing

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:09.840
for quite some time now,

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.050
can you flesh out a little
more what's CPE's work

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:13.973
and central area of focus look like?

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:17.840
- Sure, thank you for that.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:19.030
Thank you for, Crystal.

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:20.280
Thank you, Jackie.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:21.113
Thank you, Immanuel.

00:13:21.113 --> 00:13:22.900
Thank you, Vanessa.

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:25.703
We've got about 40 other folk
on the call, thank all y'all.

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:29.360
There used to be a pretty pithy way

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:32.550
to try to explain what
CPE did nine months ago.

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.350
We said we work with communities
inside of law enforcement

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:37.883
to make them less deadly, less racist.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.670
What we didn't say was
that the ultimate goal

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.510
was to make them smaller.

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.040
So, now I say that, plus
we work with communities

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.440
to make them smaller
outside of law enforcement.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:52.710
The way that we're known to function

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:56.346
is we go into police departments
after asking communities,

00:13:56.346 --> 00:13:57.440
all right, so what is it

00:13:57.440 --> 00:13:59.890
that is your biggest set of concerns?

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:02.210
We get data from the police
department, combine it

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:05.383
with other psych data
that we collect ourselves

00:14:05.383 --> 00:14:08.040
and/or census and crime data,

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.750
and that allows us to
draw a map essentially,

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:12.380
not just the racial disparities

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:14.940
and things like juvenile
detention, arrests,

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:18.590
use of force, stops, but the portion

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:21.440
of those disparities that
can reasonably be traced back

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:24.380
to law enforcement 'cause
the idea here is that

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:26.470
if black folks are let's
say four times more likely

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:28.230
to have force used on
them than white folks

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:32.050
in a given community, a skeptical chief

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.230
will come along and say
but that's just poverty.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:37.100
That's just, what about
black and black crime?

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.810
And even if we don't assume

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:43.140
that that's a bad faith argument,
which it almost always is,

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:44.450
it's reasonable to go in and say,

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:45.550
all right, so if law
enforcers are supposed

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:49.470
to go where crime goes, let's
just take that as a given,

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:50.790
then that should add influence

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:53.930
and it turns out when you
start doing just the basics,

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:57.730
the most conservative tests
that argument falls flat

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:00.640
to explain a large
portion of the variance.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:03.610
So, you simply defang
the argument and say,

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:05.150
do you have anything else?

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:06.490
If you don't and you would like

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:09.680
to make this better
here's where you can go.

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:10.900
The work that we're doing outside

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:12.200
of law enforcement now follows basically

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:13.690
the same principles, right?

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:16.480
Law enforcement says,
essentially in every sort

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:19.223
of municipality, every public hearing,

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:23.320
Department of Public Health
says, here's why you need us,

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:24.480
please keep funding us.

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:25.930
Private education says,
here's why you need us,

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:27.080
please keep funding us,

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:30.430
and law enforcement says,
fund us or you going to die.

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:33.540
They've not had to
justify why they're there.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:35.610
So, we're able to go in and
say, here are the things

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.507
that law enforcement does,
here's what they claim to do

00:15:37.507 --> 00:15:40.400
and they don't, y'all
make a decision community

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:41.980
but now you have access to it

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:45.180
in a way the law enforcement
usually has not provided.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:46.900
The reason we do it that way

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:49.820
is in part because as a scholar,

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:52.780
it's difficult for me, I
mean, also with this face,

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:55.900
it's just difficult for me to
be in front of folks saying

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:57.530
let's get activated over X, Y, and Z.

00:15:57.530 --> 00:15:59.130
That's not where my talents are.

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:02.630
But I have seen from
the inside growing up,

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.080
you know, from West
Philadelphia to the suburbs,

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:08.650
I've seen from the inside that
the temperature in the room

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:10.480
was it was making it impossible

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:11.940
for folks who would read on stuff

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:13.700
to get anything moved forward.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:15.580
And so, the analytics make it possible

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:18.230
to turn down the temperature
and move things forward.

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:21.570
That's most of what we've done.

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:25.350
And in this moment, we're
excited to be moving

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:28.690
to decenter policing as
part of public safety.

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:30.050
That's what activists were able to do

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:33.043
in the last nine months that
I hope we don't go back.

00:16:36.290 --> 00:16:37.620
You're on mute, there we go.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:39.420
- Thank you for that Professor Goff.

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:41.900
So, like, you know, I know
you mentioned that CPE,

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:44.130
you know, has historically
worked closely with the police

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:47.320
and, you know, and as you
had friends sort of working

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:48.890
with inside the system,

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:50.703
and so, you know, I'm wondering,

00:16:52.090 --> 00:16:55.490
you know, sort of how,
what led to that decision

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:57.590
and as you said how, you know,

00:16:57.590 --> 00:16:59.720
has this current moment
shifted that to sort of

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:02.800
that de-centering in these conversations,

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:04.190
especially because as you know people

00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:06.340
have grown increasingly
skeptical of sort of work

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:09.270
that works directly with police.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:10.900
- So, I mean, I wanna push back both

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:14.930
on part of the opening framing
and part of that question

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:16.773
while I embrace it at the same time.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.010
So, the reason and I'll start by answering

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:21.040
the question directly.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:22.990
The reason I started working
inside a police department

00:17:22.990 --> 00:17:24.893
is 'cause I got bullied into it.

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:27.930
That's not an excuse, I'm
not blaming nobody else.

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:31.740
I literally met a woman who challenged me

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:33.490
to instead of working
in laboratory settings,

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:35.200
I'm a social psychologist
by training, right,

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:36.210
and so for those of you who don't know

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:37.810
the discipline of social psychology,

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:39.720
our gig is that we lie to undergraduates

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:42.123
and then see what we do
and write papers about it.

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:44.270
Like I see people shaking their heads,

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:47.500
but like, no that's really,
that is a lot of what we do

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:49.930
and I never felt that was comfortable

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:53.340
for me to try and generalize
from, you know, undergraduates

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:55.690
who are at, particularly
at elite institutions.

00:17:56.612 --> 00:17:57.780
And so I wanted to be out in the field

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:00.243
and she said, " Look, come
into the police department.

00:18:00.243 --> 00:18:03.060
" Do the same stuff
out here in the field."

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:04.830
And she was coming out of a
town hall meeting a couple

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:06.710
of years prior to that engagement

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:09.660
where it was after the
murder of Paul Childs,

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:13.850
who was a 15 year old, not
mentally challenged black boy

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:17.390
who was, he was killed in his
own home in Denver, Colorado.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:20.100
She went to a meeting
as a black woman police

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:23.310
at the time captain I
believe, went to this meeting

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:24.837
and had another black woman ask her,

00:18:24.837 --> 00:18:27.587
"Do you train law enforcement
to kill young black men?"

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:30.313
Now I wanna be clear.

00:18:30.313 --> 00:18:32.440
In a public context that's
a really easy question.

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:35.900
You say, no, of course not and
I'm so sorry for your loss.

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:36.733
The brave thing to do

00:18:36.733 --> 00:18:38.710
is to actually take the
question seriously and she did.

00:18:38.710 --> 00:18:40.970
She said, " I actually don't know."

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:43.180
So, she came to me and
said, I have this question

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:44.573
and nobody will try and ask it with me

00:18:44.573 --> 00:18:47.090
or try and give me an answer.

00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:49.410
And once you got in and
you saw what the power

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:54.410
of public safety was and
could be and I don't mean

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:58.810
that in the good way, when
you see the terrifying power

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:01.520
of police unions in these contexts

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:02.730
and the ways in which folks

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:06.520
who are set up to run municipalities

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:12.210
have no idea how their
systems of public safety work.

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:13.530
Folks who've had consent decrees,

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:16.940
so DOJ has come in, done an
investigation, adjudicated them

00:19:16.940 --> 00:19:20.900
to have had a pattern and
a practice that was wrong,

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:23.603
and the local mayor still
has no idea how it runs.

00:19:24.830 --> 00:19:26.343
It was not conceivable to me

00:19:26.343 --> 00:19:31.030
that in my productive adult
years I was gonna see a country

00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:33.630
where you could say, burn
it down, start over again.

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:38.050
So, the goal was to produce systems

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:40.380
that would point towards the need

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:42.870
to take law enforcement out of places

00:19:42.870 --> 00:19:47.240
where they could not ever be
doing anything good, right?

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:49.840
The solution to homelessness
can never be a badge and a gun,

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:52.250
you better get a roof and four walls.

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:53.730
The solution like people talk about,

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:54.810
even now I'm seeing calls

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:56.250
for more critical incident training,

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:58.340
you cannot train law enforcement

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:01.030
to be the best first responders
to mental health crises.

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:02.170
You just can't do it.

00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:03.383
It's the wrong tool.

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:06.400
But if you can't call for that directly

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.520
though you can say it out loud,

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:11.030
if that's not a productive political path

00:20:11.030 --> 00:20:11.880
then what are the things

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:14.760
that allow for us to
move in that direction?

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:17.610
And actually tracking
racism in the same way

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:19.980
that they tracked crime
and allowing departments

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:21.930
to hold themselves accountable to that,

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:25.070
that seemed like a tool
that when every, like first

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:28.360
it was just do a study, give
feedback to a department

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:31.130
but in every city that was
fundamentally the thing

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:32.550
that the most radical activists

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:35.070
and the sort of everyday
traditional activists

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:35.960
were asking for.

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:37.860
Give us a way to know how bad it is

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.150
so we can hold them accountable for it.

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:42.450
And that's how we got it set up.

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:46.130
So, when you say that people
have become increasingly

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:50.000
or there's increasing, that
feels like a simplification

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.590
that I wanna make sure that
we get specific about it

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:54.880
'cause we have the space to do that here.

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:57.580
There is an ecosystem that
is rising that is ascendant

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:02.260
that has great and legitimate skepticism

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:05.510
of I think a broad range of reform

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:08.280
and having been in the space for a while,

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:12.310
I can tell you directly
as I said in '04, in '08

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:15.770
and in front of Congress
in 2010 and 2012, too much

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:18.770
of the folks working in
policing, it's a grift.

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:22.660
The folks who do implicit
bias training by and large

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:25.080
are doing, they get paid doing it,

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:26.530
they do not consult the scientist

00:21:26.530 --> 00:21:28.150
though they incite us and the damn stuff,

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:30.760
and they don't wanna know
whether or not it works.

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:32.610
And the way I know is that
we've offered for free

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:34.940
to assess whether or not it
changes behavior in the streets

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:36.260
and folks patted on the head.

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:39.240
I literally got called boy
the first time I suggested

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:40.580
that that be something that happens.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:42.753
But back when I had
hair is a long time ago.

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:45.643
So, that's fair,

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:51.150
but what's been happening
over the last four years

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:55.640
which is incremental and is
not aimed at the full scope

00:21:55.640 --> 00:22:00.620
of the problem, it also hasn't
been scaled or tried honestly

00:22:00.620 --> 00:22:03.300
and to lump those things
together to say that the folks

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:06.400
who are responsive to
what is still a majority

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:10.130
of community activists,
black folks who had diagnosed

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:13.350
the space as abolition is not possible,

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:16.100
what do we do to build so
that it can be possible

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:18.590
to lump that in with groups of folks

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:20.330
who are retired law enforcement

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:21.960
who's part of their processes

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:23.970
is to give money back into law enforcement

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:26.680
who make a profit off of doing it

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:28.250
and don't hold themselves accountable

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:29.610
to actual community goal.

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:34.610
That feels not just sort of not honest,

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:36.840
but it feels kind of dangerous to me

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:42.760
'cause I look forward to, not
like anticipation and hoping,

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:46.060
but I'm looking forward to a time

00:22:46.060 --> 00:22:49.293
when the backlash is
weaponized against this moment,

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:53.590
and if we don't have the
tools to do the bold stuff

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:58.320
and the incremental stuff, I
fear we will walk backwards

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:00.700
'cause it's not like
the concept of abolition

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:02.660
and the concept of dismantling

00:23:02.660 --> 00:23:05.440
the carceral state is new, right?

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:06.860
It is way more popular now.

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:10.010
It is way more mainstream now
than it's ever been before

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:13.350
and that's fantastic, but
it gets blotted out in part

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:15.383
because of weaponized white supremacy.

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:19.130
So, that's my pushback on that.

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:21.370
The last part of the question
was how it's changed stuff

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:24.610
and I mean, you said it in
reflection to what I'm saying.

00:23:24.610 --> 00:23:26.960
We felt we had to do public safety

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:29.390
through the powers that existed

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:31.290
and we no longer feel
like that's the only path

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:32.950
and that is an incredible relief.

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:36.020
So, we have de-centered going
through police departments

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:38.610
to do our work as an organization,

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:41.450
and that's an exciting
path to be able to talk

00:23:41.450 --> 00:23:44.483
about budgets out loud is
an exciting development.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:49.210
It's an acceleration of the
work that we've been doing,

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:51.530
but it's accelerating like
I said, it's not possible

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:53.670
without the nine months
of street organizing.

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:54.633
- Yeah, for sure.

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:56.947
Yeah, I mean, thank you for that response

00:23:56.947 --> 00:23:59.100
and I think the piece around reform

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:00.400
at least for me what's been helpful

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:03.040
is thinking about Mariame
Kaba and Critical Resistance,

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:06.097
how they frame reformist reforms
and non-reformist reforms,

00:24:06.097 --> 00:24:07.920
you know, specifically reformist reforms

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:12.230
as reforms that provide more
resources, legitimacy sort

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:14.660
of capital to institutions in,

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:17.520
you know, make them more
permanent and expand them

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:21.030
and non-reformist reforms
that seek to reduce the scope

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:25.410
and sort of reliance and
resources around policing

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:26.640
in everyday life.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:30.330
And so, that's how I try
to sort of tease that out.

00:24:30.330 --> 00:24:32.637
And so, but you mentioned
the funding piece

00:24:32.637 --> 00:24:36.280
and I know that CPE has released
a roadmap around funding

00:24:36.280 --> 00:24:38.710
and I'm interested in the roadmap

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:41.270
and also if you wanna talk
more about the concept

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:42.633
for justice tool.

00:24:43.790 --> 00:24:44.623
- Yeah, sure.

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:50.460
So, let me see if I can,
let me take Kaba to roadmap

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:52.880
to CompStat, does that work?

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:54.410
- Yeah.
- Okay.

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:57.930
So, I appreciate, I mean, in
all things I appreciate Mariame

00:24:57.930 --> 00:24:59.770
but I appreciate the
distinction she's making.

00:24:59.770 --> 00:25:02.910
I wish that we had a language just to talk

00:25:02.910 --> 00:25:05.630
I think a little bit more honestly

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:07.920
about what that distinction
breaks down into.

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:09.680
And I'm not saying claiming
that she's being dishonest

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:11.010
in any way, shape or form,

00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:13.730
but I don't think that talking
about non-reformist reform

00:25:13.730 --> 00:25:17.330
and reformist reform cuts deep enough

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:19.130
to what the problems are.

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:21.830
There are people who position
and there are reforms

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:24.110
that position themselves at fixing things

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:26.040
with no theory of change.

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:28.890
They have no idea what it would look like

00:25:28.890 --> 00:25:30.070
if they were fully successful.

00:25:30.070 --> 00:25:33.780
It's just an idea to
be part of the process.

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:36.620
And those folks who say I
believe that this is the goal

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:39.960
and this is how these set of
interventions move us closer

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:41.840
to the goal, right?

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:44.030
That feels to me a little
bit more cutting nature

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:47.030
at the joints than these
two different blocks.

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:49.084
Now we can disagree about what the goal is

00:25:49.084 --> 00:25:50.730
but there are folks who are serious

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:53.820
about I want my work to move
us in a particular direction

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:55.810
and they're folks who are much less so.

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:59.560
And as all things when you
have $115 billion on the table

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:02.010
there are way more folks
interested in getting paid

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:04.810
than there are folks interested
in making change, right?

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:09.410
But that to me is a
right set of distinctions

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:11.100
and one that's been going on in this space

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:14.550
for the folks who've been
doing it for a while, right?

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:16.760
I don't know that we're
capturing that right now

00:26:16.760 --> 00:26:17.593
in this moment.

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:20.560
The reason why we put out the roadmap

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:23.520
in the way we did the roadmap
was, I think we put it out

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:26.940
in July and the reason was,

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:29.580
so there was the public
lynching of George Floyd

00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:34.580
and obviously that sparked
huge unrest, hundreds of days

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:38.693
of protest in this country and
across the globe in solidarity.

00:26:41.095 --> 00:26:41.928
And what happened shortly

00:26:41.928 --> 00:26:45.630
thereafter, Minneapolis City
Council in collaboration

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:48.620
with activists in Minneapolis

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:51.633
who they by the way hadn't
been listening to previously,

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:56.340
they called for the defunding
which in the way that they did

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:58.190
it would have been a full dismantling

00:26:58.190 --> 00:27:00.120
of their police department

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:02.350
and right before they went
on air to announce it,

00:27:02.350 --> 00:27:03.840
how many had been
reported over the weekend

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:05.040
'cause they met on a Saturday?

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:07.490
Right before they went on
air to announce it on Monday,

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:10.400
they called and asked
if we would take part

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:14.340
and I said, look, if Messi
wants to do it, I'm with him.

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:15.173
Let's go.

00:27:16.420 --> 00:27:18.490
So, they went on air and we went on air

00:27:18.490 --> 00:27:21.590
and that night we got
I think 350 phone calls

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:23.333
from folks asking how.

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:26.970
And then for the course
of the next two weeks

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:29.980
they were in the 1500 phone calls.

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:33.320
There were police departments,
sheriff's departments,

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:36.620
state and local electeds,
activists looking

00:27:36.620 --> 00:27:37.770
for a way to get there

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:41.660
and there was no way we had bandwidth,

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:42.530
you know, two years ago

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:44.670
we were an organization of 17 people.

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:47.463
We are as of today, I think
around 70, seven zero.

00:27:48.858 --> 00:27:50.540
We're not gonna deal
with 1500 jurisdictions,

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:52.160
ain't gonna happen.

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:53.980
So, I'm thinking, what
is it that we can do

00:27:53.980 --> 00:27:55.510
that can provide a map for the folks

00:27:55.510 --> 00:27:58.710
who we're not working
with directly and closely?

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:01.210
So, we took the stuff that
we knew one should do,

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:04.730
that we would do that you
could give away combined

00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:06.710
with the research that's out there

00:28:06.710 --> 00:28:07.980
that would make some kind of sense.

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:09.923
I have a regret in that
which is, we worked with...

00:28:09.923 --> 00:28:12.970
So, the five steps where
essentially, doing a 911 survey,

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:15.010
where people calling for police resources?

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:17.740
Second, do an officer discretionary survey

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:18.820
where officers go in

00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:20.590
when they're not getting
directed by the public, right?

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:23.870
And that should give you a
sense of what are they managing,

00:28:23.870 --> 00:28:26.420
like how are they engaging directly?

00:28:26.420 --> 00:28:28.810
The third step I believe
was focus deterrence.

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:30.110
We gonna get back to that.

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:33.590
The fourth step was identifying the places

00:28:33.590 --> 00:28:36.290
that were burdened either by crime

00:28:36.290 --> 00:28:39.730
like so, by street violence
or by police presence

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:41.380
in the absence of crime,
'cause you can do that

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:42.930
if you've done the first two steps

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:45.890
and the fifth step is
essentially providing resources

00:28:45.890 --> 00:28:47.520
in there.

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:48.720
We include a focus deterrence

00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:51.030
'cause there's so much consensus

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:54.100
that it's fewer police resources,

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:56.060
that it should if it's done

00:28:56.060 --> 00:28:58.730
the appropriate way
foreground community power

00:28:58.730 --> 00:28:59.840
to identify the folks that are like,

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:00.840
these are the violence committers.

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.690
We wanna engage them directly

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:05.470
and it provides an off-ramp
for violence before

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:06.870
there's a punitive response.

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:08.920
I wish we hadn't done that.

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:10.520
The reason I wish we hadn't done that

00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:14.160
is because it still relies on
law enforcement and punishment

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:15.840
as the root out of violence

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:19.210
despite the fact that there are reductions

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:20.970
to both of those systems
within focus deterrence.

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:24.330
I wish what we had thought of upfront

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:27.720
was the same thing, same
exact focused deterrence

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:30.120
but with universal basic
income instead of punishment

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:32.190
as the response which is
where we essentially (mumbles)

00:29:32.190 --> 00:29:33.040
in four and five.

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:36.230
No excuses on that, like we
put out a public document.

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:37.070
I wrote the darn thing

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:39.433
along with Jeff Asher and other folks.

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:44.560
But the idea was to provide
a little bit of release val

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:46.770
'cause what we were hearing from people

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:48.030
when we said we can't get back to you

00:29:48.030 --> 00:29:49.983
is like, okay, if you can't do it,

00:29:49.983 --> 00:29:51.180
then we're just gonna wait.

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:53.870
We're just gonna do nothing
and when the country's on fire

00:29:53.870 --> 00:29:55.840
we wanted folks to be able to take a step

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:57.930
that looked like a plan where
they could put some data

00:29:57.930 --> 00:30:01.280
in front of themselves to calm themselves

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:02.450
so that they could do some things

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:03.990
that were significantly beyond

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:06.543
what they could have imagined
just a month earlier.

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:11.311
Not the perfect way to go but
that was the goal in that.

00:30:11.311 --> 00:30:12.144
And you asked about concept

00:30:12.144 --> 00:30:12.990
but I saw you take yourself off here,

00:30:12.990 --> 00:30:14.340
you wanna ask me a question
before I get the cops out

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:15.173
or are you good?

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:19.670
- Maybe to, 'cause for folks who,

00:30:19.670 --> 00:30:21.240
you know, might not know
about focus deterrence

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:23.860
I wanna just sort of, I think
you painted a good picture

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:25.900
and I just wanna sort of
like branch out even more

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:29.090
is like a large part is
sort of in that punitive

00:30:30.070 --> 00:30:32.670
sort of approach is this
idea of pulling levers

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:34.420
and it's not only sort of individuals

00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:36.940
but in the entire social networks

00:30:36.940 --> 00:30:39.550
that people are sort of like a part of,

00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:42.770
that are seen as part
of particular groups.

00:30:42.770 --> 00:30:45.720
And, you know, the idea
is that if one person,

00:30:45.720 --> 00:30:48.040
you know, engages in
violence at the entire group

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:50.690
will sort of, you know, will face

00:30:50.690 --> 00:30:51.970
the sort of full scope of the law

00:30:51.970 --> 00:30:52.980
and often they bring in state

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:57.557
and federal level prosecutors
in that work and so.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:00.800
- So, let's drill down in that though

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:04.750
'cause that is bad
focus deterrence, right?

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:05.583
I'm saying, I don't think

00:31:05.583 --> 00:31:07.260
we should have focus
deterrence in there at all,

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:08.657
but between Gary Slutkin

00:31:08.657 --> 00:31:10.700
who has cure violence on the one hand,

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:14.300
David Kennedy has got National
Network for Safe Communities.

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:17.200
Pastor Mike, who does focus
deterrence out of what used

00:31:17.200 --> 00:31:20.390
to be the Pico Network, there
are different versions of it.

00:31:20.390 --> 00:31:24.740
That's the worst cop, where
you have a drag net for folks

00:31:24.740 --> 00:31:26.830
who are connected in a social network

00:31:26.830 --> 00:31:29.860
with an individual who
commits an act of violence.

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:32.720
The other forms of of
focus deterrence are,

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:33.840
you have a group of folks

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:35.980
who are prone to commit violence according

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:38.430
to past behavior, right?

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:40.550
They're in a network where
we should identify them

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:42.770
as vulnerable to violence
'cause that's really

00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:44.740
the way that those networks are working

00:31:44.740 --> 00:31:46.530
and you call them in, community calls them

00:31:46.530 --> 00:31:48.540
and says, we know that you
guys are the most likely,

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:50.970
who are blinded by violence, if you do it

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:52.530
and you don't go these other routes

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:54.900
we're gonna call the cops on
you and you in particular.

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:56.750
Again, wish we hadn't done it

00:31:56.750 --> 00:31:58.197
but that's a different
kind of focus deterrence

00:31:58.197 --> 00:32:00.420
and it kind of says
everybody in the network

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:03.810
if one goes you all go which is, I mean,

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:07.170
that's unconstitutional and
gross in lots of different ways.

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:08.610
- Yeah, and I'm, you know, I'm glad

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:09.443
that part of what you mentioned

00:32:09.443 --> 00:32:13.720
is sort of a shift away from,
you know, sort of a focus

00:32:13.720 --> 00:32:16.380
on punishment to a focus on
sort of what does it look like

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:19.590
to assess needs and providing
resources as a model

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:23.280
as opposed to this sort of
intense sort of punitive.

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:25.710
But, you know, in terms of
the concept for justice,

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:27.780
you know, the specific
thing that I was wondering

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:29.680
if you we're able to talk more about

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:32.220
is sort of how poverty in reports

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:36.180
of crime becomes sort of
a part of the assessment

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:41.180
of whether in terms of police
deployment, sort of, yeah,

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:42.870
just talking more about what goes

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:45.160
into the metrics of
that concept for justice

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:47.680
and what that looks like.

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:49.477
- Okay, I definitely can explain it

00:32:49.477 --> 00:32:51.590
but I'm not quite sure I
understood the question.

00:32:51.590 --> 00:32:53.050
- Well, I guess if we can start by saying,

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:54.370
what is the concept for justice?

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:56.610
- Okay, so for folks who are
not in the policing space

00:32:56.610 --> 00:32:59.650
you may have heard CompStat
and it has a terrible rep.

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:03.450
It has a terrible rep only
because it deserves it, okay?

00:33:03.450 --> 00:33:06.170
So, what it is at its root,

00:33:06.170 --> 00:33:09.260
what it was initially
meant to be, y'all know

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:12.270
how Target knows when you
and your partner get pregnant

00:33:12.270 --> 00:33:14.430
and how Google knows
what you're searching for

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:15.850
and why you have to
clear the search history

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:17.680
'cause they looked at the pattern

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:20.380
and that was able to predict
what's happening going forward.

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:24.130
And the argument is you can
do that with crime, okay?

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:29.130
And you can to a degree, right,
look at past reported crimes

00:33:29.350 --> 00:33:32.170
and say, these are areas
and these are individuals

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:34.220
who are likely to commit again.

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:35.580
Problem, of course, with that is,

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:37.070
and this is not minority report.

00:33:37.070 --> 00:33:38.227
We'll get into like, if we want to

00:33:38.227 --> 00:33:40.283
we can get into algorithm justice stuff.

00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:43.570
Problems with that there is no such thing

00:33:43.570 --> 00:33:47.100
as crime statistics, full stop.

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:48.720
That's not an ideological
thing that's scripted.

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:50.700
There's literally no such
thing as crime statistics.

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:52.930
There's reported crime statistics.

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:56.600
So, in New Haven, I don't
know this for a fact

00:33:56.600 --> 00:33:58.350
but I'd be willing to bet

00:33:58.350 --> 00:34:00.560
that the place that has the highest rate

00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:04.170
of underage drinking is the dorms.

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:06.860
I'd also be willing to bet that the place

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:08.150
that has the highest rate

00:34:08.150 --> 00:34:11.140
of what is still
considered illicit drug use

00:34:11.140 --> 00:34:12.610
is the campus.

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:14.330
That's not me trying to
knock out the campus,

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:16.160
that's just saying any university town

00:34:16.160 --> 00:34:17.430
that has got a university

00:34:17.430 --> 00:34:19.560
is usually the site of the highest level

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:23.320
of what are still considered
illegal narcotics consumption.

00:34:23.320 --> 00:34:25.290
But you don't see law
enforcement hanging out waiting

00:34:25.290 --> 00:34:27.770
to bust up a dorm party.

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:31.170
We have decided that that's
not illegal in the same way

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:32.870
as the same activities are

00:34:32.870 --> 00:34:34.810
in what's considered to
be an open-air market.

00:34:34.810 --> 00:34:36.290
That's the problem with CompStat,

00:34:36.290 --> 00:34:38.500
is that for the stats
folks it's indoctrinated,

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:40.780
you got the bias already baked in

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:44.020
'cause you're only stopping
black people in the first place.

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:46.660
Indoctrinated doesn't however
mean it's a useless tool,

00:34:46.660 --> 00:34:49.560
it just means there are
the problems with it.

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:52.190
Beyond that, this is
now just a concept thing

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:54.920
and I'll explain why we get
the concept just a second.

00:34:54.920 --> 00:34:57.720
Beyond that it started getting
used as a management tool

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:00.180
in a way that forget
about bringing down crime,

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:03.080
you had to contribute
to an increased number

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:07.460
of stops week over week, month
over month, year over year

00:35:07.460 --> 00:35:10.750
to show that we're doing
anything at all, right,

00:35:10.750 --> 00:35:13.260
'cause if we're not
driving the numbers down

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:16.210
then we're not doing anything
is the logic behind that.

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:18.250
Of course, that's ludicrous.

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:20.510
So, the green law enforcement
can do anything positive.

00:35:20.510 --> 00:35:23.090
They surely can't do
the same sets of things

00:35:23.090 --> 00:35:25.380
as appropriate education, healthcare,

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:27.660
employment, housing, right?

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:29.470
So, if they need to be the drivers

00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:31.100
of get to get down to zero,

00:35:31.100 --> 00:35:32.350
but at some point you're cutting corners

00:35:32.350 --> 00:35:34.830
and you're just doing stuff that corrodes

00:35:34.830 --> 00:35:36.600
the fabric of democracy.

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:38.140
That's why it has a bad rap.

00:35:38.140 --> 00:35:41.100
But it's earliest idea is
that we can measure a thing

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:43.860
and hold ourselves accountable
to it and that helps.

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:45.920
And there is some evidence

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:47.580
that when law enforcement
holds themselves accountable

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:50.680
to crime, the crime goes
down not just through

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:51.990
the measurements of law enforcement.

00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:56.050
So, not just through self-report
and that kind of mess.

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:57.820
So, the idea is since about 80%

00:35:57.820 --> 00:35:59.680
of major city law
enforcement uses some system

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:01.660
like CompStat and I
think that number is low,

00:36:01.660 --> 00:36:02.730
like the vast majority

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:05.480
of major city law enforcement
uses a system like CompStat,

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:09.963
it's part of the DNA of how
they do patrol and deployment.

00:36:11.470 --> 00:36:13.330
Why would they not wanna measure justice

00:36:13.330 --> 00:36:14.430
instead of just crime?

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:16.880
And if you can put it into the systems

00:36:16.880 --> 00:36:18.970
in the same kind of way, right,

00:36:18.970 --> 00:36:21.360
and give it over to communities,

00:36:21.360 --> 00:36:22.650
so the community is
holding them accountable

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:25.100
not just for crime, but for disparity

00:36:25.100 --> 00:36:27.420
and their portion of disparity,

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:31.270
you can dissenter crime
as the way that we talk

00:36:31.270 --> 00:36:32.610
about public safety

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:35.890
and you can recenter
community values there.

00:36:35.890 --> 00:36:39.483
Let me say this last thing
in terms of how this works.

00:36:40.610 --> 00:36:43.050
All the folks who wanted to do the things

00:36:43.050 --> 00:36:45.450
that are now getting called
for within law enforcement,

00:36:45.450 --> 00:36:47.040
the police chiefs who wanted to

00:36:47.917 --> 00:36:50.850
get officers out of public
health, out of mental health,

00:36:50.850 --> 00:36:53.120
out of substance abuse, out
of the homelessness racket,

00:36:53.120 --> 00:36:54.820
get them out of all of that stuff,

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:58.493
their biggest threat
is that crime goes up.

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:00.410
And what happens there is that

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:01.243
even in the most vulnerable communities,

00:37:01.243 --> 00:37:03.720
they're like crime is up
what you doing get out

00:37:03.720 --> 00:37:07.400
'cause we only hold police
accountable for crime.

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:09.970
There literally wasn't a
mechanism to hold them accountable

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:12.130
for something else statistically speaking

00:37:12.130 --> 00:37:14.170
in a way that was easy and obvious

00:37:15.038 --> 00:37:16.660
for electeds or for communities.

00:37:16.660 --> 00:37:18.870
So, even in the communities
that their biggest concern

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:20.510
about law enforcement was racism,

00:37:20.510 --> 00:37:23.810
they would call for the firing
of a chief in a crime spot.

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:26.410
So, the goal was to produce a product

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:28.900
that would allow for communities
to have a different metric

00:37:28.900 --> 00:37:31.660
to change the conversation
and again, de-center crime

00:37:31.660 --> 00:37:33.650
as the end all be all of what was going on

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:35.100
inside the law enforcement.

00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:37.420
Now Phil you had a
question about the metrics,

00:37:37.420 --> 00:37:39.110
maybe this is the right time to ask about

00:37:39.110 --> 00:37:40.365
how the quantitative stuff works

00:37:40.365 --> 00:37:42.630
and the theoretical elements,
that's where you were going.

00:37:42.630 --> 00:37:45.610
- Yeah, so in terms of what
goes into determining, right,

00:37:45.610 --> 00:37:49.890
like this is, sort of this
model of concept for justice,

00:37:49.890 --> 00:37:52.070
how do you determine
if something is unjust

00:37:52.070 --> 00:37:53.210
or like if there's patterns

00:37:53.210 --> 00:37:56.280
in a particular area disproportionate?

00:37:56.280 --> 00:37:58.190
- So it's a great question
and it's something

00:37:58.190 --> 00:38:01.100
that I feel like the
public sort of face of this

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:02.590
does a bad job of explaining.

00:38:02.590 --> 00:38:03.740
So, I appreciate getting a chance

00:38:03.740 --> 00:38:07.390
to try and analyze it this way.

00:38:07.390 --> 00:38:09.580
CompStat for just the way we set it up

00:38:09.580 --> 00:38:13.593
is not designed to do the
best science possible.

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:15.603
It's not.

00:38:16.490 --> 00:38:18.080
Here's what I mean.

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:22.227
The best science possible in
the traditional academic sense

00:38:22.227 --> 00:38:23.350
and I don't even mean
the best science possible

00:38:23.350 --> 00:38:25.920
what I would wanna do,
where that would be trying

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.137
to get real close down
to causality, right?

00:38:29.137 --> 00:38:31.060
And we have no interest

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:32.530
in trying to reduce this to causality.

00:38:32.530 --> 00:38:37.140
The reason being the data are so crappy.

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:39.180
We couldn't possibly do that at scale.

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:41.120
It's just not possible.

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:44.750
What we're doing is
descriptive statistics,

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:45.980
and what we're really doing

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:50.470
is disallowing one form of argument.

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:51.950
It is the most common form of argument,

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:54.663
so it's incredibly valuable
to my mind, just allow it.

00:38:55.600 --> 00:38:58.730
If I'm a chief and you say,
look at these disparities,

00:38:58.730 --> 00:39:00.400
you're clearly racist

00:39:00.400 --> 00:39:02.380
and I'm remotely literate in this stuff,

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:03.213
I say, I'm not racist.

00:39:03.213 --> 00:39:05.440
I'm going where crime is,
I'm going where poverty is.

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:08.160
We see it and we hear
it all the time, right,

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:12.750
like I had to spend probably
three weeks beating back

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:17.230
the dumbest analysis of
police behavioral data

00:39:17.230 --> 00:39:19.080
in the history of christiandom saying,

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:20.460
there's no bias in policing.

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:21.910
Are you kidding me?

00:39:21.910 --> 00:39:25.000
How do you do that this summer
for three f*ing weeks?

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:27.670
But it's partially because the argument

00:39:27.670 --> 00:39:29.610
is so robust that people are like, well,

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:32.910
it does seem kind of
reasonable, just that.

00:39:32.910 --> 00:39:36.677
So, if I take crime and
I take poverty, right,

00:39:36.677 --> 00:39:40.250
and I take it off the table, right,

00:39:40.250 --> 00:39:42.730
that doesn't actually, that's
not a good way of saying

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:44.760
that police don't
contribute to crime itself.

00:39:44.760 --> 00:39:46.630
I actually have research
selling that they do.

00:39:46.630 --> 00:39:47.463
It's not a good way of saying

00:39:47.463 --> 00:39:49.360
the police don't contribute
to poverty, right,

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:50.950
I don't yet have that research in print,

00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:52.520
we're working on that too.

00:39:52.520 --> 00:39:56.690
But it does say shut up
about poverty and crime

00:39:56.690 --> 00:39:58.523
as an excuse that it's not on y'all.

00:39:59.590 --> 00:40:01.133
It takes it off the table.

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:04.610
And so, what CompStat for
justice is set up to do

00:40:04.610 --> 00:40:08.530
is to say even if you were
right, and crime and poverty

00:40:08.530 --> 00:40:10.100
are entirely out of your hands

00:40:10.100 --> 00:40:11.850
and they don't overlap at all

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:13.960
with the things that you're
doing to communities,

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:16.550
if that were true this is the portion

00:40:16.550 --> 00:40:18.290
that would still be you.

00:40:18.290 --> 00:40:20.890
Do you have any arguments about that?

00:40:20.890 --> 00:40:22.170
And in every city where we work,

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:23.810
they're like, oh I guess,
I don't, I hadn't thought

00:40:23.810 --> 00:40:25.410
that you were gonna come
back with anything else

00:40:25.410 --> 00:40:28.400
even though in every city we work, we do.

00:40:28.400 --> 00:40:30.180
So, think about it in philosophical terms

00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:31.380
as eminent critique.

00:40:31.380 --> 00:40:34.270
I'm gonna take for granted
the arguments that you've got

00:40:34.270 --> 00:40:35.830
and you're still full of it.

00:40:35.830 --> 00:40:39.190
You still can't justify
that the racial disparities

00:40:39.190 --> 00:40:42.360
that we see, they're not,
like poverty and crime

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:44.370
are not sufficient to explain them.

00:40:44.370 --> 00:40:47.170
In fact, your policies and your behaviors

00:40:47.170 --> 00:40:50.580
have to factor in at a significant level,

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:51.780
and at that point what you've done

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:53.510
is you've removed a bunch of the barriers

00:40:53.510 --> 00:40:57.947
to public safety officials
being involved in change,

00:40:57.947 --> 00:40:59.180
and you say, okay, so now we're

00:40:59.180 --> 00:41:01.390
gonna be held accountable to that.

00:41:01.390 --> 00:41:03.580
It raises the specter

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:06.690
that you could get in good
faith, folks getting that portion

00:41:06.690 --> 00:41:08.170
that they're responsible down to zero

00:41:08.170 --> 00:41:11.600
and you for sure will not
have not solved the problem.

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:14.310
I would love to get to that day

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:15.770
'cause we're nowhere near

00:41:15.770 --> 00:41:18.210
in the most progressive
most liberal cities

00:41:18.210 --> 00:41:21.250
that have the smallest
per capita police forces

00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:25.860
and the largest and best teeth
around civilian oversight.

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:29.100
So, it's a tool to de-center crime

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:31.530
and de-center those arguments

00:41:31.530 --> 00:41:33.430
in the production of public safety.

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:34.890
That's the way it was conceptualized.

00:41:34.890 --> 00:41:36.920
- Yeah, and I mean, of
course, as you mentioned

00:41:36.920 --> 00:41:39.630
that as we do know that there's, you know,

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:41.590
the problem of indoctrinated
that all those things

00:41:41.590 --> 00:41:43.970
are sort of wrapped up in one another,

00:41:43.970 --> 00:41:44.803
- Absolutely.

00:41:44.803 --> 00:41:46.310
- Yeah, and my fear is
sort of like, you know,

00:41:46.310 --> 00:41:48.660
with that as, sort of like
was learning more about it

00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:50.500
is like this idea that if,

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:53.860
you know, police then say,
well, we solved this gap,

00:41:53.860 --> 00:41:55.890
so we're only responding to, you know,

00:41:55.890 --> 00:41:58.247
we're only going here because
it's this level of poverty

00:41:58.247 --> 00:42:01.190
and this level of crime,
and it can reinforce

00:42:01.190 --> 00:42:04.260
those sorts of logics that
they should be responding.

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:06.010
And so, you know, one of the things

00:42:06.010 --> 00:42:07.080
that came to my mind was sort of

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:11.830
if there are alternative
ways to also sort of include,

00:42:11.830 --> 00:42:13.270
you know, like if you're
responding to this

00:42:13.270 --> 00:42:16.150
because, you know, these
levels of race or poverty

00:42:16.150 --> 00:42:18.610
that this is also like unjust, you know,

00:42:18.610 --> 00:42:21.900
and so, yeah, so it's (mumbles).

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:24.077
- Yes, so every city where we roll it out,

00:42:24.077 --> 00:42:25.578
one of the nice things

00:42:25.578 --> 00:42:27.010
is to be able to talk to folks, right?

00:42:27.010 --> 00:42:27.843
So, they're like, yeah,

00:42:27.843 --> 00:42:29.160
so we're only responsible for this 40%.

00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:31.060
I was like, so first of all 40%

00:42:31.060 --> 00:42:34.610
of a 15 to one disparity,
please don't smile.

00:42:34.610 --> 00:42:36.250
That's not a good thing.

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:39.060
And secondly, you wanna claim the 40%,

00:42:39.060 --> 00:42:41.210
this 30% that's you're
being driven by poverty,

00:42:41.210 --> 00:42:42.760
I just wanna be clear
that you said out loud

00:42:42.760 --> 00:42:45.160
you're okay to go and arrest poverty

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:46.860
'cause poverty should be punished

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:49.180
with the criminal legal system.

00:42:49.180 --> 00:42:50.450
So, once you start saying it out loud,

00:42:50.450 --> 00:42:52.650
you end up with portions of that disparity

00:42:52.650 --> 00:42:55.450
that are pretty significant,
that are morally objectionable

00:42:55.450 --> 00:42:58.610
to pretty much everybody
in the room, right.

00:42:58.610 --> 00:43:02.240
Now, you could take it
and flip it and say,

00:43:02.240 --> 00:43:04.650
the only thing you're
responsible is this portion,

00:43:04.650 --> 00:43:06.530
let's get that as close
to zero as possible

00:43:06.530 --> 00:43:09.480
and that's used to shut up any resistance

00:43:09.480 --> 00:43:11.550
to the ways in which public safety

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:13.960
gets used to further
marginalized communities.

00:43:13.960 --> 00:43:17.453
You absolutely could use it
to do that and you shouldn't.

00:43:18.290 --> 00:43:22.900
But most of the tools that
I see that have broad,

00:43:22.900 --> 00:43:25.430
in my mind have broad capacity

00:43:25.430 --> 00:43:28.120
to reduce the footprint of law enforcement

00:43:28.120 --> 00:43:31.460
are easily tripped up
in the exact same way.

00:43:31.460 --> 00:43:33.730
So, I think what we have
to do is get real specific

00:43:33.730 --> 00:43:35.450
on how we want the stuff to get used,

00:43:35.450 --> 00:43:37.100
how we don't, get real loud

00:43:37.100 --> 00:43:38.820
when it's getting used the wrong way

00:43:38.820 --> 00:43:40.990
and talk about the ways in
which it's actually counter

00:43:40.990 --> 00:43:43.570
to both the moral valence
that we wanna put on this

00:43:43.570 --> 00:43:45.370
and the science on it.

00:43:45.370 --> 00:43:48.976
I don't think anybody can justify 30%

00:43:48.976 --> 00:43:52.590
of the racial disparity
here is driven by poverty

00:43:52.590 --> 00:43:55.941
as somehow police are doing
the right thing, right,

00:43:55.941 --> 00:43:56.900
like I don't think anybody

00:43:56.900 --> 00:43:59.430
with a decent sense of just moral center

00:43:59.430 --> 00:44:02.083
can make that argument out
loud once it's made clear.

00:44:03.020 --> 00:44:06.220
So, there is the good faith
and the bad faith argument,

00:44:06.220 --> 00:44:07.300
and then there's the appropriateness

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:08.840
of the tool towards the direction

00:44:08.840 --> 00:44:12.210
that believe you and I are
trying to get together,

00:44:12.210 --> 00:44:15.370
which is the full dismantling
of the state apparatus

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:17.090
that was put into place primarily

00:44:17.090 --> 00:44:21.820
to regulate the liberty
of black bodies, right?

00:44:21.820 --> 00:44:24.810
- Yeah, so, yeah, I mean,
let's, I know you mentioned

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:28.230
this before too and so
let's talk about Minneapolis

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:31.810
and sort of about a lot
of the progressive reforms

00:44:31.810 --> 00:44:33.820
that were, have historically sort of been

00:44:33.820 --> 00:44:35.300
at least politically attainable

00:44:35.300 --> 00:44:37.050
and sort of that hadn't been implemented

00:44:37.050 --> 00:44:37.883
in different places.

00:44:37.883 --> 00:44:42.130
And, you know, CPE has
worked in Minneapolis,

00:44:42.130 --> 00:44:43.810
in concert with the National Institute

00:44:43.810 --> 00:44:45.460
for Community Trust and Justice

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:47.620
in the years leading up to 2020

00:44:47.620 --> 00:44:50.040
and worked collectively into
another great deal of resources

00:44:50.040 --> 00:44:53.200
to implement some of those popular reforms

00:44:53.200 --> 00:44:57.960
of procedural justice, sort of
focus implicit bias training,

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:00.510
intervene policies,
reconciliation efforts,

00:45:00.510 --> 00:45:02.730
focus on diversity and community policing.

00:45:02.730 --> 00:45:06.039
And, you know, you shared
in the past that it's,

00:45:06.039 --> 00:45:07.330
you know, at least
according to certain metrics

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:11.180
it's reduced particular
metrics of use of force by 18%,

00:45:11.180 --> 00:45:13.660
but we all know in 2020
Minneapolis became the site

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:15.690
of an uprising over the
murder of George Floyd

00:45:15.690 --> 00:45:17.280
that's spread throughout the country.

00:45:17.280 --> 00:45:20.230
And so, you know, what
does it say about the,

00:45:20.230 --> 00:45:23.070
you know, about sort
of these popular forms

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:25.490
and just we'd love to hear
more about your thoughts

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:27.700
in how to sort of situate that.

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:29.570
- Yeah, and so and here again I want folks

00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:32.370
to do a closer reading of
what happened in Minneapolis,

00:45:32.370 --> 00:45:35.037
really really do 'cause there is grift

00:45:35.037 --> 00:45:36.770
that's been going on in Minneapolis

00:45:36.770 --> 00:45:40.090
but there was an alarm sounded in 2015

00:45:40.090 --> 00:45:42.213
about the culture in Minneapolis PD.

00:45:43.450 --> 00:45:44.417
You know, it's, well-known Bob Kroll

00:45:44.417 --> 00:45:48.320
who is the current union lead
what his affiliations are

00:45:48.320 --> 00:45:50.070
in terms of white supremacy organizations.

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:52.810
It's well-known who did, and didn't go to,

00:45:52.810 --> 00:45:55.243
and who abided by and
didn't abide by policy.

00:45:56.420 --> 00:45:58.980
Let's go back to the
frame of the question.

00:45:58.980 --> 00:46:00.490
You talked about popular reform

00:46:00.490 --> 00:46:02.610
and you talked about
significant resources.

00:46:02.610 --> 00:46:03.753
I wanna challenge that

00:46:03.753 --> 00:46:07.480
because I think it's not accurate, okay?

00:46:07.480 --> 00:46:09.949
In terms of resources, for
those of you who are asking

00:46:09.949 --> 00:46:13.970
what he's asking about,
the National Initiative

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:16.689
for Building Community Trust and Justice

00:46:16.689 --> 00:46:19.690
'cause DOJ named it and DOJ
is terrible at naming things.

00:46:19.690 --> 00:46:23.130
The idea was to take three
things that we knew worked

00:46:24.440 --> 00:46:27.430
in laboratories and see whether
or not it worked in cities

00:46:27.430 --> 00:46:28.263
so that it could scale.

00:46:28.263 --> 00:46:30.193
It was a pilot program, okay?

00:46:31.489 --> 00:46:34.801
It's about $4.75 million which
I know I got humanists here

00:46:34.801 --> 00:46:37.650
and that is a lot of money,
right, in the abstract.

00:46:37.650 --> 00:46:39.040
Let me break down what that really means.

00:46:39.040 --> 00:46:41.700
There were six cities over three years

00:46:41.700 --> 00:46:43.650
across four organizations.

00:46:43.650 --> 00:46:47.630
So, for quick math, it's
$75,000 per organization

00:46:47.630 --> 00:46:49.180
per city per year.

00:46:49.180 --> 00:46:53.250
That means each of us hired
one recent college graduate

00:46:53.250 --> 00:46:55.270
and gave them bad health insurance.

00:46:55.270 --> 00:46:58.020
That's functionally what it does, okay.

00:46:58.020 --> 00:46:59.750
During that time there were improvements

00:46:59.750 --> 00:47:01.240
in measures of public trust,

00:47:01.240 --> 00:47:03.110
that's kind of an amazing thing

00:47:03.110 --> 00:47:04.700
'cause we didn't do a PR campaign.

00:47:04.700 --> 00:47:07.250
There was no, hey, look at
how much better we're getting.

00:47:07.250 --> 00:47:09.480
Black communities in
particular felt their police

00:47:09.480 --> 00:47:12.681
were less racist in the way
that they were being treated.

00:47:12.681 --> 00:47:16.510
That feels like a really
good metric to me, right,

00:47:16.510 --> 00:47:19.120
not just the trust, but
that they were less racist.

00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:21.950
There were reductions in
some, but not all the cities

00:47:21.950 --> 00:47:25.330
in use of force that were
proximately attributable

00:47:25.330 --> 00:47:28.040
to some of the interventions
and there were reductions

00:47:28.040 --> 00:47:30.730
in racial disparities
in that use of force.

00:47:30.730 --> 00:47:35.730
It was a modest gain from a
massively underfunded initiative

00:47:35.810 --> 00:47:40.200
which was by far the largest
investment in reforms

00:47:40.200 --> 00:47:44.520
that had become popular, we
started in real time like 2016.

00:47:45.608 --> 00:47:48.750
I think the grant was to 2015
but in real time it was 2016.

00:47:48.750 --> 00:47:52.190
They had become popular the year before.

00:47:52.190 --> 00:47:53.930
So, we talked about popular reforms

00:47:53.930 --> 00:47:55.340
like stuff that was
popular for five years,

00:47:55.340 --> 00:47:56.760
that's a long time in pop music

00:47:56.760 --> 00:47:57.620
but it's not a long time

00:47:57.620 --> 00:48:00.090
in terms of government
intervention, right?

00:48:00.090 --> 00:48:04.010
They are popular now because
of the last five years

00:48:04.010 --> 00:48:05.460
where folks are like, oh,
there's a thing off the shelf,

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:08.080
let's go do it, right?

00:48:08.080 --> 00:48:09.200
They have not been funded,

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:10.670
they've not been skilled and to the degree

00:48:10.670 --> 00:48:11.503
that they've been rolled out much

00:48:11.503 --> 00:48:13.290
like community policing, right?

00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:15.080
It started out as a thing
where you should have folks

00:48:15.080 --> 00:48:16.990
in the community actually
policing themselves.

00:48:16.990 --> 00:48:20.620
Community policing is
not a policing reform.

00:48:20.620 --> 00:48:23.380
It's a community empowerment
reform at its base.

00:48:23.380 --> 00:48:25.520
It got turned into, okay,
so we should have community,

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:27.740
we should have police in the community

00:48:27.740 --> 00:48:29.250
and then it's, we should have a unit

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:30.740
that knows how to talk to folks

00:48:30.740 --> 00:48:33.140
and dance off competitions
and be a PR campaign.

00:48:34.090 --> 00:48:36.170
So yeah, if that's what
community policing is it's trash,

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:38.020
it's garbage, right?

00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:40.110
I said from the beginning
that implicit bias training

00:48:40.110 --> 00:48:44.130
by itself is everything
about that is a disaster.

00:48:44.130 --> 00:48:45.860
As we were publishing and popularizing

00:48:45.860 --> 00:48:47.540
and police (mumbles)
people, somebody, some idiot

00:48:47.540 --> 00:48:49.350
is gonna turn this into just a training

00:48:49.350 --> 00:48:51.160
and think you could just
do it, take it off the box

00:48:51.160 --> 00:48:51.993
and be done with it.

00:48:51.993 --> 00:48:54.940
We wrote that when we first
came out with the science.

00:48:54.940 --> 00:48:57.480
That's not to say that you can't look

00:48:57.480 --> 00:48:59.784
at the situations people are in

00:48:59.784 --> 00:49:03.150
and try and reduce the
degree to which their biases

00:49:03.150 --> 00:49:05.790
are informing their behaviors, right?

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:08.760
And then measure whether or
not the behaviors change.

00:49:08.760 --> 00:49:11.360
So, what does it mean in
the context of Minneapolis?

00:49:11.360 --> 00:49:14.590
The backdrop is these things
have been to the degree

00:49:14.590 --> 00:49:15.485
that they're popular.

00:49:15.485 --> 00:49:17.400
They've been popular for 15 seconds.

00:49:17.400 --> 00:49:18.470
To the degree that they've taken money,

00:49:18.470 --> 00:49:20.050
it's massively less than what's needed

00:49:20.050 --> 00:49:22.620
to scale something like this
to see a kind of influence

00:49:22.620 --> 00:49:25.770
especially in the context
of $115 billion industry.

00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:28.170
But in terms of what it
means about Minneapolis,

00:49:30.270 --> 00:49:32.750
I mean, I said this to you Phil
when we spoke the last time

00:49:32.750 --> 00:49:35.420
in our pre-conversation,
if you do this work

00:49:35.420 --> 00:49:38.873
and you don't get up every
day expecting to fail that,

00:49:40.280 --> 00:49:42.480
I don't know that you're
right for the work.

00:49:43.681 --> 00:49:46.500
But if you get up thinking
that these systems

00:49:46.500 --> 00:49:49.890
that were set up to brutalize
the sons and daughters

00:49:49.890 --> 00:49:54.750
of formerly enslaved folks
are gonna fall to you today,

00:49:54.750 --> 00:49:56.810
it's kind of optimism that I think is,

00:49:56.810 --> 00:49:58.910
it borders on irresponsible and dangerous.

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:02.910
I personally sent a memo
to the chief of police

00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:07.910
in Minneapolis in end of March
beginning of April saying,

00:50:08.040 --> 00:50:10.343
you know, you have a subculture.

00:50:14.630 --> 00:50:16.327
- I think you're on mute.

00:50:16.327 --> 00:50:18.150
- Oh no, I'm not on mute.

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:18.983
You can hear me now?

00:50:18.983 --> 00:50:20.360
Okay.

00:50:20.360 --> 00:50:22.730
So, I sent a memo to
Arradondo, the chief police

00:50:22.730 --> 00:50:25.483
in Minneapolis, end of
March beginning of April,

00:50:26.862 --> 00:50:28.850
you know you got, and by the
way we had stopped working

00:50:28.850 --> 00:50:29.753
with them at that.

00:50:29.753 --> 00:50:31.197
(Indistinct)

00:50:31.197 --> 00:50:32.930
But you know, you've got a subculture

00:50:32.930 --> 00:50:34.110
that is explicitly white supremacists.

00:50:34.110 --> 00:50:36.760
You sue them including naming Bob Kroll,

00:50:36.760 --> 00:50:38.630
the head of the police union, sue them

00:50:38.630 --> 00:50:41.370
for racial discrimination
and one before your chief

00:50:42.717 --> 00:50:46.323
and you have had no success
in rooting out that culture.

00:50:47.340 --> 00:50:51.590
That means everything we've
done here can get flipped

00:50:51.590 --> 00:50:53.520
in one incident.

00:50:53.520 --> 00:50:55.540
Set that thing, right as
COVID was setting examples

00:50:55.540 --> 00:50:56.850
'cause I was really worried
what we're gonna do,

00:50:56.850 --> 00:50:59.200
is we were gonna criminalize
folks who were non-compliant,

00:50:59.200 --> 00:51:00.033
which really meant

00:51:00.033 --> 00:51:01.720
we weren't criminalized
black folks, right?

00:51:01.720 --> 00:51:03.630
I didn't want law enforcement involved

00:51:03.630 --> 00:51:05.580
in the enforcement of COVID regulations

00:51:05.580 --> 00:51:08.030
and social distancing and
if we got to mask wear.

00:51:09.030 --> 00:51:10.210
So, what it says about Minneapolis

00:51:10.210 --> 00:51:12.530
is that it's like every other city.

00:51:12.530 --> 00:51:15.500
There is no amount of inside game

00:51:15.500 --> 00:51:18.440
that is going to fix
the mission of policing

00:51:19.280 --> 00:51:21.010
which at the founding of this country,

00:51:21.010 --> 00:51:22.790
when you first have law enforcement

00:51:22.790 --> 00:51:25.097
was to regulate black bodies, right

00:51:25.097 --> 00:51:27.280
and at no point in time
did we take an intermission

00:51:27.280 --> 00:51:28.253
and say, " Okay, that was messed up.

00:51:28.253 --> 00:51:29.703
" We should stop doing that."

00:51:30.730 --> 00:51:33.083
There is no inside game
that's gonna do that.

00:51:33.950 --> 00:51:35.360
It's what I knew going in.

00:51:35.360 --> 00:51:36.860
It's what I know today.

00:51:36.860 --> 00:51:38.850
It's why I'm relieved to be able to have,

00:51:38.850 --> 00:51:42.500
I was the inside game to other
folks outside game, right?

00:51:42.500 --> 00:51:44.100
I now get to be inside and outside.

00:51:44.100 --> 00:51:46.710
That's a relief living
your life more honestly,

00:51:46.710 --> 00:51:47.543
more directly.

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:51.300
But what I'd hope we don't do

00:51:51.300 --> 00:51:53.220
is what I keep hearing folks say,

00:51:53.220 --> 00:51:55.823
we now know reforms don't work.

00:51:56.970 --> 00:52:01.500
To what end and with
which communities, right?

00:52:01.500 --> 00:52:04.930
There are reforms that do the
thing they're supposed to do.

00:52:04.930 --> 00:52:08.270
Ain't no reform that set up, right,

00:52:08.270 --> 00:52:10.820
to fully dismantle tomorrow.

00:52:10.820 --> 00:52:13.960
Performance harm reduction
and harm reduction is valuable

00:52:13.960 --> 00:52:15.240
within a context.

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:16.910
We look to talk to any
public health person.

00:52:16.910 --> 00:52:18.520
Sometimes the best you
can do is harm reduction

00:52:18.520 --> 00:52:20.563
especially in the context of addiction.

00:52:21.520 --> 00:52:22.920
And do I think that we're addicted

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:24.970
to a militarized public safety force?

00:52:24.970 --> 00:52:26.183
Of course we are.

00:52:27.330 --> 00:52:30.580
So, you can't tell me the
best thing going forward

00:52:30.580 --> 00:52:32.910
is to abandon harm reduction

00:52:32.910 --> 00:52:36.080
in service of anti addictions at work.

00:52:36.080 --> 00:52:37.580
They have to go together.

00:52:37.580 --> 00:52:39.870
The problem comes when harm reduction

00:52:39.870 --> 00:52:43.113
is in the way of getting
us out of the addiction.

00:52:43.960 --> 00:52:46.810
And that's what needs to
happen to reform folks

00:52:46.810 --> 00:52:49.280
who are still in that
harm reduction space.

00:52:49.280 --> 00:52:51.230
That's why, I mean, that's
what I think is going on

00:52:51.230 --> 00:52:53.040
in Minneapolis right now.

00:52:53.040 --> 00:52:55.470
They had us in there, we were out.

00:52:55.470 --> 00:52:56.600
They asked us to come back.

00:52:56.600 --> 00:52:57.910
They've asked us to come back again.

00:52:57.910 --> 00:53:01.010
Now the process through the city council

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:04.740
has been very publicly not what
they wanted it to be, right,

00:53:04.740 --> 00:53:07.060
'cause there's going to
need to be harm reduction

00:53:07.060 --> 00:53:09.130
on the way to where we're going.

00:53:09.130 --> 00:53:12.580
It just needs to not get in
the way of where we're going.

00:53:12.580 --> 00:53:14.050
- Yeah, and that's something

00:53:14.050 --> 00:53:16.340
I wanna sort of build more on it.

00:53:16.340 --> 00:53:20.410
So, when you say in the way
what sorts of approaches

00:53:20.410 --> 00:53:23.460
have you employed in
terms of CPE to make sure

00:53:23.460 --> 00:53:24.530
that you don't get in the way

00:53:24.530 --> 00:53:28.010
of those more sort of bold
transformative shifts?

00:53:28.010 --> 00:53:29.540
- I'll say previous of this moment,

00:53:29.540 --> 00:53:31.520
not enough probably, right

00:53:31.520 --> 00:53:32.390
'cause previous to the moment

00:53:32.390 --> 00:53:36.260
like I'm black, I'm from
Philly, graduated high school

00:53:36.260 --> 00:53:38.733
with Muniz's son, the youngest son.

00:53:39.600 --> 00:53:41.550
I've been in the space and doing this work

00:53:41.550 --> 00:53:43.600
and screaming at police
chiefs for a long enough time

00:53:43.600 --> 00:53:45.500
and nobody really questioned anything.

00:53:46.910 --> 00:53:49.410
There are ways in which
folks who work internally

00:53:49.410 --> 00:53:51.970
get in the way that, two in particular

00:53:53.060 --> 00:53:57.170
that are incredibly damaging
that I feel like we did okay,

00:53:57.170 --> 00:54:01.470
but we weren't as intentional
as we started being May 25th.

00:54:01.470 --> 00:54:04.463
The two ways are, we call it
the shield and the offering.

00:54:05.620 --> 00:54:08.430
So, there are communities where folks,

00:54:08.430 --> 00:54:10.710
they take the roadmap
that CPE put together

00:54:10.710 --> 00:54:13.210
and they're like, CPE said
this, we're gonna do this.

00:54:13.210 --> 00:54:14.730
These are the experts.

00:54:14.730 --> 00:54:15.950
Now we don't work there.

00:54:15.950 --> 00:54:17.090
We had no conversations.

00:54:17.090 --> 00:54:19.010
They just downloaded it from the website.

00:54:19.010 --> 00:54:19.947
But there are local organizers

00:54:19.947 --> 00:54:23.530
who are like, well, we
already did a 911 survey.

00:54:23.530 --> 00:54:24.530
Are you just going to throw it out?

00:54:24.530 --> 00:54:26.140
Like, yeah, yeah, we're
going to do the CPE way.

00:54:26.140 --> 00:54:27.940
Yep, we already did that.

00:54:27.940 --> 00:54:29.750
But essentially because we set it

00:54:30.820 --> 00:54:32.250
for any national organization that set it

00:54:32.250 --> 00:54:34.180
that has credibility outside

00:54:34.180 --> 00:54:35.660
of whatever, small, local municipality

00:54:35.660 --> 00:54:39.350
it gets used as the way to say,
hold on, not what you want,

00:54:39.350 --> 00:54:40.453
what these folks know.

00:54:41.440 --> 00:54:43.540
And so what needs to
happen in those contexts

00:54:43.540 --> 00:54:45.160
is all of our materials are being revamped

00:54:45.160 --> 00:54:48.840
going into 2021 to say we
cannot be used as a shield.

00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:51.280
It's against the science, it's
against our ethics, right?

00:54:51.280 --> 00:54:54.820
And we're working more proactively
with organizers in cities

00:54:54.820 --> 00:54:57.850
where we don't work to make
sure that they understand

00:54:57.850 --> 00:54:59.670
if we're ever used as a
shield, get us on the phone.

00:54:59.670 --> 00:55:00.940
We will go on local television

00:55:00.940 --> 00:55:03.060
and say, that's MBS, we're not with it.

00:55:03.060 --> 00:55:05.455
That's the wrong way to use our stuff.

00:55:05.455 --> 00:55:06.288
That's the first way.

00:55:06.288 --> 00:55:07.121
That's the shield.

00:55:07.121 --> 00:55:10.270
For off-ramp in some
ways is more insidious

00:55:10.270 --> 00:55:11.103
and more dangerous, right?

00:55:11.103 --> 00:55:12.890
It's part of like what
people will talk about

00:55:12.890 --> 00:55:14.130
as a participation trap.

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:16.210
And Phil this is a problem
when you and I get together

00:55:16.210 --> 00:55:18.440
it's not just a battle
of one L versus two L's

00:55:18.440 --> 00:55:20.040
is that we're, this is inside baseball.

00:55:20.040 --> 00:55:22.610
So, let me back up and do
participation trap, right,

00:55:22.610 --> 00:55:25.620
so folks who are not in
movement spaces understand

00:55:25.620 --> 00:55:28.200
that language and then I'll
go back to the off-ramp.

00:55:28.200 --> 00:55:29.033
For those who are not familiar

00:55:29.033 --> 00:55:31.740
with the term participation
trap is the idea

00:55:31.740 --> 00:55:33.790
that you are standing in resistance,

00:55:33.790 --> 00:55:36.330
you're protesting, you are
saying something needs to change,

00:55:36.330 --> 00:55:37.543
and then that institution says,

00:55:37.543 --> 00:55:39.567
" Great, let's do it together."

00:55:40.410 --> 00:55:42.220
And you say, "Okay."

00:55:42.220 --> 00:55:45.570
And by virtue of being in participation

00:55:45.570 --> 00:55:47.780
of being in collaboration
with the institution,

00:55:47.780 --> 00:55:50.500
the milk toast half
measure that is coming out,

00:55:50.500 --> 00:55:52.910
your name somehow has attached to it

00:55:52.910 --> 00:55:54.610
or they'll say, oh, well, they
weren't really collaborative,

00:55:54.610 --> 00:55:55.690
they weren't really serious

00:55:55.690 --> 00:55:59.210
so that even if the invitation
to participation is a trap

00:56:00.550 --> 00:56:03.400
that derails the movement towards

00:56:03.400 --> 00:56:05.690
the kind of progress
folks are trying to make.

00:56:05.690 --> 00:56:07.770
That's the participation trap.

00:56:07.770 --> 00:56:10.890
The risk for national
organizations like CPE

00:56:10.890 --> 00:56:13.500
is that we will name a set of things

00:56:13.500 --> 00:56:15.710
that this is where you can get to

00:56:15.710 --> 00:56:17.990
and that becomes the final
destination for folks

00:56:17.990 --> 00:56:20.569
as opposed to steps along the way.

00:56:20.569 --> 00:56:22.470
And so, they say, okay,
we did what CPE said,

00:56:22.470 --> 00:56:24.630
now we get be done.

00:56:24.630 --> 00:56:25.580
It's a common problem

00:56:25.580 --> 00:56:27.880
in negotiations politically all the time.

00:56:27.880 --> 00:56:30.090
So, this summer, some of
you may have been following

00:56:30.090 --> 00:56:33.410
the Justice in Policing Act
was put forward by a number

00:56:33.410 --> 00:56:35.810
of Dems in the House, it
passed out of the House,

00:56:35.810 --> 00:56:38.020
didn't get introduced on
the floor of the Senate

00:56:38.020 --> 00:56:40.630
but there was great
consternation in hand-wringing

00:56:40.630 --> 00:56:44.680
'cause it was eight
principles that were good,

00:56:44.680 --> 00:56:46.230
they weren't abolitionists
by any stretch of imagination

00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:47.063
but there were good.

00:56:47.063 --> 00:56:48.450
They were gonna help something

00:56:48.450 --> 00:56:49.950
and lots of folks wanted more.

00:56:50.980 --> 00:56:51.890
And then there were other folks who said,

00:56:51.890 --> 00:56:53.060
if we ask for that much,

00:56:53.060 --> 00:56:55.180
it doesn't get to the floor of the Senate

00:56:55.180 --> 00:56:56.280
and we'll get nothing.

00:56:58.210 --> 00:56:59.400
What are you supposed to do?

00:56:59.400 --> 00:57:02.200
Ask for all the things
that you want, right,

00:57:02.200 --> 00:57:03.620
or negotiate against yourself

00:57:03.620 --> 00:57:06.360
and guarantee that you get last stand?

00:57:06.360 --> 00:57:07.770
The problem with national organizations

00:57:07.770 --> 00:57:10.780
is because there are places
where we're not working.

00:57:10.780 --> 00:57:13.210
We can be used as that off-ramp

00:57:13.210 --> 00:57:14.960
to get less than what local organizers

00:57:14.960 --> 00:57:17.830
have decided they want to push for, right?

00:57:17.830 --> 00:57:19.960
And so, to articulate that as a principle

00:57:19.960 --> 00:57:22.810
that we should not be in the
way of where local organizers

00:57:22.810 --> 00:57:25.510
are ultimately going to
again work proactively

00:57:25.510 --> 00:57:27.670
in the places where we don't already work,

00:57:27.670 --> 00:57:30.490
to say you should use
our voice to push back

00:57:30.490 --> 00:57:32.390
against folks trying to use science

00:57:32.390 --> 00:57:35.110
to say, there is no
bias, training is enough,

00:57:35.110 --> 00:57:37.400
training is anything in this space.

00:57:37.400 --> 00:57:39.230
It's not nothing, but people use it like

00:57:39.230 --> 00:57:41.790
it's the end all be all lever
as if individual officers

00:57:41.790 --> 00:57:46.350
are the problem or God forbid
that these set of solutions

00:57:46.350 --> 00:57:49.873
which we suggest are
useful are the end game.

00:57:51.360 --> 00:57:56.330
But it is a hard thing to
do science in the abstract

00:57:56.330 --> 00:57:59.040
in a world that isn't abstract,

00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:01.370
to do science where we're
still fumbling towards

00:58:01.370 --> 00:58:05.110
these are the right sets
of ways to quantify this

00:58:05.110 --> 00:58:05.980
while people are needing

00:58:05.980 --> 00:58:08.290
to make policy decisions right away.

00:58:08.290 --> 00:58:09.630
So, there are gonna be times

00:58:09.630 --> 00:58:11.930
when we are getting used as a shield,

00:58:11.930 --> 00:58:13.540
and I hate to say this to all other people

00:58:13.540 --> 00:58:17.020
who are in this space, you
too will be used that way

00:58:17.020 --> 00:58:19.770
and as an off-ramp, and we
didn't get the attention to it

00:58:19.770 --> 00:58:21.820
so we couldn't intervene to prevent that.

00:58:22.820 --> 00:58:24.910
I don't think there's a
way to prevent it a 100%.

00:58:24.910 --> 00:58:26.730
I think that what can be done

00:58:26.730 --> 00:58:29.210
is we need to explicitly
say, don't do that,

00:58:29.210 --> 00:58:30.340
don't use us that way

00:58:30.340 --> 00:58:32.500
and then we need to devote our resources

00:58:32.500 --> 00:58:35.450
to intervening when folks
are misusing our work

00:58:35.450 --> 00:58:37.123
to be in the way that way.

00:58:39.666 --> 00:58:41.900
- Yeah, so, you know,
recently in (indistinct)

00:58:41.900 --> 00:58:44.750
you shared, I'm black man
centering black communities.

00:58:44.750 --> 00:58:46.630
That means abolishing
systems of racial violence

00:58:46.630 --> 00:58:48.560
and reforming public
institutions that can be made

00:58:48.560 --> 00:58:51.220
to reflect the full
realization of black freedom,

00:58:51.220 --> 00:58:52.970
is policing a system of racial bias

00:58:52.970 --> 00:58:54.820
and needs to be abolished
or a public institution

00:58:54.820 --> 00:58:56.433
that needs to be reformed?

00:58:58.740 --> 00:59:03.240
- So, the easy answer for
me is it's the first, right?

00:59:03.240 --> 00:59:06.043
The system that we have, nope.

00:59:07.970 --> 00:59:08.843
The more complicated answer is then

00:59:08.843 --> 00:59:12.400
what do you do with
community violence, right,

00:59:12.400 --> 00:59:14.970
like this is the conversation
I wish we were having

00:59:14.970 --> 00:59:16.260
amongst folks who are dedicated

00:59:16.260 --> 00:59:19.030
to what I think is mostly common calls.

00:59:19.030 --> 00:59:21.820
When someone is engaged in violence

00:59:21.820 --> 00:59:25.340
who can intervene in a
way that can be trusted

00:59:25.340 --> 00:59:27.350
when you're new in town, right,

00:59:27.350 --> 00:59:30.350
When you're scared because your
abuser is also your beloved.

00:59:31.390 --> 00:59:34.050
Who are the folks that intervene then?

00:59:34.050 --> 00:59:38.150
Community violence
interrupters can be fantastic.

00:59:38.150 --> 00:59:40.293
They cannot be everything.

00:59:41.160 --> 00:59:42.737
When I talked to Erica
Ford about this stuff,

00:59:42.737 --> 00:59:47.163
she like, I'm in that space
and we can't do all the things

00:59:47.163 --> 00:59:48.950
that the community needs
to keep itself safe

00:59:48.950 --> 00:59:51.900
especially 'cause we're not at a space

00:59:51.900 --> 00:59:53.710
where there are the resources

00:59:53.710 --> 00:59:55.660
to prevent so many of
those forms of violence

00:59:55.660 --> 00:59:56.663
in the first place.

00:59:57.640 --> 01:00:02.450
So, I am interested in what we
build up that isn't policing,

01:00:02.450 --> 01:00:05.270
that isn't named that way,
that doesn't have that legacy.

01:00:05.270 --> 01:00:07.990
I'm invested in helping
to figure that out.

01:00:07.990 --> 01:00:10.116
That's what my science
has always been about

01:00:10.116 --> 01:00:12.308
and will continue to be about.

01:00:12.308 --> 01:00:15.913
And I feel like there's a
lot of ideological alignment

01:00:17.020 --> 01:00:19.320
from all the folks across the spectrum.

01:00:19.320 --> 01:00:22.523
I said, many of them,
(indistinct) on the end state,

01:00:23.459 --> 01:00:24.910
but how do we get there?

01:00:24.910 --> 01:00:27.100
How do we keep the most
vulnerable folks safe

01:00:27.100 --> 01:00:29.540
between now and the there?

01:00:29.540 --> 01:00:32.160
All the work that I've ever
done inside of law enforcement

01:00:32.160 --> 01:00:35.650
was with the goal of
giving communities power

01:00:35.650 --> 01:00:38.200
to decide how they were
gonna keep themselves safe.

01:00:38.200 --> 01:00:39.410
I still stand by that.

01:00:39.410 --> 01:00:41.510
As many mistakes as I feel
like I've made along the way,

01:00:41.510 --> 01:00:44.840
most of the mistakes are
enrolled out in framing,

01:00:44.840 --> 01:00:45.700
in relationship.

01:00:45.700 --> 01:00:49.193
I still stand by the
science and the tactics.

01:00:50.865 --> 01:00:55.050
But how we get from here to a future state

01:00:55.050 --> 01:00:57.400
that centers the most
vulnerable communities,

01:00:57.400 --> 01:00:59.710
gives them dignity and authority

01:00:59.710 --> 01:01:01.978
to determine how they stay safe

01:01:01.978 --> 01:01:03.570
and still allows for the fact that

01:01:03.570 --> 01:01:07.550
in vulnerable communities
there will be violence,

01:01:07.550 --> 01:01:09.923
I don't know exactly what that looks like.

01:01:10.947 --> 01:01:12.100
And so, when we talk about re-imagining,

01:01:12.100 --> 01:01:15.090
this is the imagination, but
I think we need to get to

01:01:15.090 --> 01:01:17.700
our best Afro-futurists
like the abolitionists

01:01:17.700 --> 01:01:20.730
that I was reading since
grad school, right?

01:01:20.730 --> 01:01:23.500
They are able to talk about
things that don't work

01:01:23.500 --> 01:01:24.397
and some things that do,

01:01:24.397 --> 01:01:27.490
and I don't feel like
I'm all the way there

01:01:27.490 --> 01:01:28.790
in terms of having a clear vision

01:01:28.790 --> 01:01:30.240
of exactly what that looks like

01:01:30.240 --> 01:01:32.110
and I know what it looks
like in the future state,

01:01:32.110 --> 01:01:35.300
but what the transition, that
change management looks like

01:01:35.300 --> 01:01:38.430
what we do between here and there

01:01:38.430 --> 01:01:40.520
'cause I don't imagine a future

01:01:40.520 --> 01:01:42.460
where there aren't public resources

01:01:42.460 --> 01:01:45.600
to intercede when violence is out of hand.

01:01:45.600 --> 01:01:47.500
I do see systems where
we don't have to treat

01:01:47.500 --> 01:01:49.870
that like punishment is the only response,

01:01:49.870 --> 01:01:52.040
but I don't see systems where
we don't have to intervene

01:01:52.040 --> 01:01:54.140
in community violence
unless we're abandoning

01:01:54.140 --> 01:01:55.620
those communities to themselves

01:01:55.620 --> 01:01:58.440
which creates its own set of problems.

01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:00.970
So, the short answer is it's the first

01:02:00.970 --> 01:02:01.950
and the second answer

01:02:01.950 --> 01:02:03.573
is I think way more complicated than that.

01:02:03.573 --> 01:02:05.270
Does that make sense?

01:02:05.270 --> 01:02:08.320
- Yeah, you know, I think
the question of violence

01:02:08.320 --> 01:02:09.960
is the one that often
comes up and, you know,

01:02:09.960 --> 01:02:13.270
we know that police don't
sort of stop it sort

01:02:13.270 --> 01:02:15.430
of in the ways and they cause
different forms of violence

01:02:15.430 --> 01:02:18.960
until the question is it's
then sort of as you mentioned

01:02:18.960 --> 01:02:21.730
is how do we build a, you know, presence

01:02:21.730 --> 01:02:22.700
and sort of alternatives.

01:02:22.700 --> 01:02:27.430
And I think there's a lot
of models that we have

01:02:27.430 --> 01:02:28.900
that can be scaled up and others

01:02:28.900 --> 01:02:30.170
that have to be experimented.

01:02:30.170 --> 01:02:31.573
Other folks have said I think, you know,

01:02:31.573 --> 01:02:34.670
there's a need to try
everything and change everything

01:02:35.690 --> 01:02:39.150
as Mariame Kaba and Ruth
Wilson Gilmore had shared.

01:02:39.150 --> 01:02:43.380
So, you know, we're
getting some questions.

01:02:43.380 --> 01:02:48.103
So, let's ask this one from Jonathan.

01:02:49.460 --> 01:02:51.000
He shares, you express pessimism

01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:54.200
about the inside game based
around relationship management

01:02:54.200 --> 01:02:55.200
with police departments

01:02:55.200 --> 01:02:57.180
and noted that implicit
bias training grifters

01:02:57.180 --> 01:02:59.010
are separate from social scientists.

01:02:59.010 --> 01:03:01.170
I'm trying to get my hands
on more use of force data

01:03:01.170 --> 01:03:03.320
but police departments have
no reason to give it to me.

01:03:03.320 --> 01:03:04.810
I'm highly critical.

01:03:04.810 --> 01:03:06.410
Would you or CPE consider committing

01:03:06.410 --> 01:03:08.730
to sharing any data sets
publicly you acquired

01:03:08.730 --> 01:03:11.370
through relationships with the
broader academic community?

01:03:11.370 --> 01:03:12.500
I worry that if scientists

01:03:12.500 --> 01:03:14.257
have to maintain good relationships
with police departments

01:03:14.257 --> 01:03:17.400
to get data, it means police
communications jobs determine

01:03:17.400 --> 01:03:19.840
what the public knows
about race and policing.

01:03:19.840 --> 01:03:22.190
And this is similar to a question I had

01:03:22.190 --> 01:03:24.320
'cause, you know, some folks
have have said, you know,

01:03:24.320 --> 01:03:28.040
the national database that
we've all built is large

01:03:28.040 --> 01:03:31.053
but it's not publicly
available and so, yeah.

01:03:32.170 --> 01:03:34.440
- Yeah, and so I'm also seeing in the chat

01:03:34.440 --> 01:03:36.380
the way in which we frame policing.

01:03:36.380 --> 01:03:39.630
Policing is violence, right,

01:03:39.630 --> 01:03:43.840
and also in black community
after black community

01:03:43.840 --> 01:03:46.440
after black community,
you will find people

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:48.970
whose lives they say are still there

01:03:48.970 --> 01:03:50.810
because they called the police.

01:03:50.810 --> 01:03:52.663
Not police black folks.

01:03:54.350 --> 01:03:57.160
Somebody on an interdict
right with violence

01:03:57.160 --> 01:04:00.630
and if it's not police,
there is a lack of clarity

01:04:00.630 --> 01:04:02.620
about what that would look like.

01:04:02.620 --> 01:04:05.160
That doesn't mean I am in
favor of keeping status quo

01:04:05.160 --> 01:04:06.080
by any stretch.

01:04:06.080 --> 01:04:09.490
I'm just saying there needs
to be more specificity

01:04:09.490 --> 01:04:12.370
as we move through that
and I'm for try everything

01:04:12.370 --> 01:04:15.440
and, it's a both and, but
that changed management,

01:04:15.440 --> 01:04:17.010
that's a beast.

01:04:17.010 --> 01:04:19.260
So, to John, and I think
it's John (indistinct).

01:04:21.435 --> 01:04:22.613
Okay, hey, how you doing?

01:04:24.000 --> 01:04:26.760
So, in terms of sharing data

01:04:26.760 --> 01:04:28.060
the way that the national justice database

01:04:28.060 --> 01:04:31.400
got to be the largest
police behavioral database

01:04:31.400 --> 01:04:34.750
in the world is that we
didn't require anybody

01:04:34.750 --> 01:04:36.520
to share anything publicly.

01:04:36.520 --> 01:04:37.840
Now to be clear we started

01:04:37.840 --> 01:04:41.563
this right after Trayvon
Martin was shot killed, right?

01:04:42.610 --> 01:04:45.030
There was no future state where I said

01:04:45.030 --> 01:04:47.710
in my productive adulthood,
anything like that,

01:04:47.710 --> 01:04:50.620
like open demand would be possible.

01:04:50.620 --> 01:04:53.500
But it was also the case that
we were already seeing folks

01:04:53.500 --> 01:04:57.240
who were using police data to
do really simplistic analysis

01:04:57.240 --> 01:04:59.200
on both for, on behalf
of the police department

01:04:59.200 --> 01:05:02.700
as if they were a stats PR
arm, folks who take money

01:05:02.700 --> 01:05:05.670
from law enforcement which
in case it's relevant,

01:05:05.670 --> 01:05:07.610
we do not for exactly that reason

01:05:07.610 --> 01:05:10.140
but also scientists who were
just not particularly critical

01:05:10.140 --> 01:05:10.993
or well-trained.

01:05:12.271 --> 01:05:13.180
And so, what we wanted to do was we wanted

01:05:13.180 --> 01:05:16.540
to make public not just
the data but the analytics,

01:05:16.540 --> 01:05:18.547
and we had a of departments
who said, " No, thank you.

01:05:18.547 --> 01:05:20.197
"We'll take this, we're super racist.

01:05:20.197 --> 01:05:22.300
"That's scary, goodbye."

01:05:22.300 --> 01:05:24.810
Other departments like
(indistinct) who made it public

01:05:24.810 --> 01:05:26.810
but I think it made it easier
for the (indistinct) collective

01:05:26.810 --> 01:05:30.050
to argue for the things
that they argued for, right?

01:05:30.050 --> 01:05:33.090
Going forward now that
we're in this new state,

01:05:33.090 --> 01:05:35.050
we are requiring that
any of the departments

01:05:35.050 --> 01:05:37.600
that come in brand new
to us, and keep in mind

01:05:37.600 --> 01:05:39.520
we have a backlog of about 250

01:05:39.520 --> 01:05:40.890
but any of the new
departments and anything

01:05:40.890 --> 01:05:42.610
that we're gonna fast
track coming up forward,

01:05:42.610 --> 01:05:44.443
they must make the analytics public.

01:05:45.700 --> 01:05:47.880
But to the degree that there's
been any kind of concerns

01:05:47.880 --> 01:05:50.800
around this, I gotta say
I have not had a bunch

01:05:50.800 --> 01:05:53.260
of folks reach out and say,
hey, can we collaborate?

01:05:53.260 --> 01:05:54.540
I have these sets of questions.

01:05:54.540 --> 01:05:56.880
Can I get access and collaboration?

01:05:56.880 --> 01:05:59.860
'Cause there is no restriction
on us bringing collaborators

01:05:59.860 --> 01:06:01.007
in, setting them up

01:06:01.007 --> 01:06:03.480
and having them do their own thing, right?

01:06:03.480 --> 01:06:04.420
We've reached out to folks

01:06:04.420 --> 01:06:05.860
and said, hey, you've had
a lot of critical things

01:06:05.860 --> 01:06:07.820
to say about this scientific approach,

01:06:07.820 --> 01:06:09.300
are you interested in access to the data

01:06:09.300 --> 01:06:10.520
that could disprove it?

01:06:10.520 --> 01:06:14.020
And I am, I'll say I'm not surprised

01:06:14.020 --> 01:06:16.630
that when we offer this to
folks who put out there,

01:06:16.630 --> 01:06:18.150
it's scientific fact that there's no bias,

01:06:18.150 --> 01:06:19.780
they don't want access to the data.

01:06:19.780 --> 01:06:21.800
They just say the data are not public

01:06:21.800 --> 01:06:23.420
and they're not responsive to our entries.

01:06:23.420 --> 01:06:24.530
Please use our data to go ahead

01:06:24.530 --> 01:06:26.530
and ask your same questions
with your same methods.

01:06:26.530 --> 01:06:28.100
So, for social scientists
who are interested in

01:06:28.100 --> 01:06:29.773
and who are hearing my voice, right,

01:06:29.773 --> 01:06:31.630
we got our capacity issues, right,

01:06:31.630 --> 01:06:34.310
we're still scaling up to make
our science team large enough

01:06:34.310 --> 01:06:37.540
to be able to be responsive,
but come get some.

01:06:37.540 --> 01:06:40.340
There's no reason for
collaboration to be off the table.

01:06:41.220 --> 01:06:42.590
For the new folks, so the short answer

01:06:42.590 --> 01:06:43.423
for the new folks coming in

01:06:43.423 --> 01:06:45.390
we're requiring them to make that public.

01:06:45.390 --> 01:06:49.410
Our MOUs require that we
can ask, we can encourage,

01:06:49.410 --> 01:06:50.940
but we can't unilaterally decide

01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:52.533
to make the old data public.

01:06:54.500 --> 01:06:56.080
Let me say one last thing on that.

01:06:56.080 --> 01:06:58.080
I started with, and I'll end with it.

01:06:58.080 --> 01:06:59.890
If you just give disparities,

01:06:59.890 --> 01:07:02.140
if you just give four
to one racial disparity

01:07:03.829 --> 01:07:06.000
with the data, just
that simple descriptive,

01:07:06.000 --> 01:07:07.620
and that's, by the way, that's nationally.

01:07:07.620 --> 01:07:08.840
Police are four times more likely

01:07:08.840 --> 01:07:11.230
to beat up black folks
than white folks, right,

01:07:11.230 --> 01:07:13.240
just benchmarked on population.

01:07:13.240 --> 01:07:15.023
If you just do that, you're
gonna get folks in the streets

01:07:15.023 --> 01:07:17.290
they're like, told you they were racist

01:07:17.290 --> 01:07:18.740
and you got to get law enforcement

01:07:18.740 --> 01:07:21.810
who are like that's not
a reasonable analysis

01:07:21.810 --> 01:07:24.510
and everybody's more entrenched
in their original position.

01:07:24.510 --> 01:07:26.920
So, for everybody who
wants access to data,

01:07:26.920 --> 01:07:28.940
I am in favor of democratizing data.

01:07:28.940 --> 01:07:30.450
I want more data.

01:07:30.450 --> 01:07:32.810
But I want, with the police
data in particular around race,

01:07:32.810 --> 01:07:34.170
I want it to come with analytics.

01:07:34.170 --> 01:07:36.150
I want it to come framed responsibly

01:07:36.150 --> 01:07:39.880
'cause otherwise what you
end up with folks being mad

01:07:39.880 --> 01:07:41.270
about what they came in thinking

01:07:41.270 --> 01:07:45.090
and no ability for change whatsoever.

01:07:45.090 --> 01:07:47.437
And I hate seeing science used that way.

01:07:50.049 --> 01:07:53.910
- Okay, we have a question
around defund the police.

01:07:53.910 --> 01:07:56.910
As we know, it's been sort
of used in various ways

01:07:56.910 --> 01:07:58.983
by different politicians.

01:08:00.090 --> 01:08:04.310
For political capital it's,
you know, also confusing.

01:08:04.310 --> 01:08:05.620
Do you find the police
has just become a thing?

01:08:05.620 --> 01:08:09.330
I know recently you shared,
you know, your thoughts that

01:08:09.330 --> 01:08:13.000
if it wasn't framed in that
way, you know, things like

01:08:13.919 --> 01:08:17.390
the surveys might've
not even been launched,

01:08:17.390 --> 01:08:19.330
but I'm wondering if you wanna talk more

01:08:19.330 --> 01:08:23.120
about sort of CPE's stand,
your thoughts around,

01:08:23.120 --> 01:08:23.953
you know, defund the police

01:08:23.953 --> 01:08:26.280
and the whole framing conversation.

01:08:26.280 --> 01:08:29.550
- Yeah, I mean, I guess maybe I'll flag.

01:08:29.550 --> 01:08:31.150
I'm speaking here not
fully on behalf of CPE.

01:08:31.150 --> 01:08:33.980
CPE is a broader
organization than just me.

01:08:33.980 --> 01:08:36.730
I get to be here as a professor,
at least I think I do.

01:08:38.240 --> 01:08:40.397
My frame on it is, this is one
of those frustrating things

01:08:40.397 --> 01:08:43.160
that I have ever seen in my lifetime.

01:08:43.160 --> 01:08:45.090
How are Democratic strategists talking

01:08:45.090 --> 01:08:47.440
about defund should have
been something different?

01:08:47.440 --> 01:08:52.320
How, did anybody who was
talking defund when it launched?

01:08:52.320 --> 01:08:55.750
Was anybody saying, and this
is an electoral strategy?

01:08:55.750 --> 01:08:57.760
That's like people, like
Democrat strategist coming out

01:08:57.760 --> 01:09:00.450
and saying, I feel like
McDonald, I'm loving it.

01:09:00.450 --> 01:09:02.570
It is not helping people vote Democrat.

01:09:02.570 --> 01:09:03.403
What?

01:09:03.403 --> 01:09:04.800
They're not related.

01:09:04.800 --> 01:09:06.490
They're not the same thing.

01:09:06.490 --> 01:09:11.420
The goal of the rallying cry
was to make concrete, a set

01:09:11.420 --> 01:09:14.053
of tactics on the other
way towards abolition.

01:09:14.053 --> 01:09:16.860
It's a modest set of recommendations

01:09:16.860 --> 01:09:19.530
to say money that you put
in towards public safety

01:09:19.530 --> 01:09:22.370
should actually go towards
things that make the public safe.

01:09:22.370 --> 01:09:25.940
And folks are arguing that
it's bad for electoral politics

01:09:25.940 --> 01:09:29.250
because particularly
Democrats have not figured out

01:09:29.250 --> 01:09:32.880
how to speak with fidelity
to black community issues

01:09:32.880 --> 01:09:36.860
at the same time that they're
appealing to white centrists.

01:09:36.860 --> 01:09:41.860
That is not activists problem
and it is mind numbing to me

01:09:42.540 --> 01:09:45.260
that we have folks who
are yelling at activists

01:09:45.260 --> 01:09:49.410
and I mean, the names and the
meanness and the nastiness

01:09:49.410 --> 01:09:53.090
as of activist somehow are
supposed to be conscripted

01:09:53.090 --> 01:09:54.610
in the same electoral project

01:09:56.210 --> 01:09:59.420
as professional Democratic operatives.

01:09:59.420 --> 01:10:02.300
That's just dumb, right, like it's just,

01:10:02.300 --> 01:10:04.770
it's irresponsibly unsophisticated

01:10:04.770 --> 01:10:08.250
and mind-numbingly illogical to me.

01:10:08.250 --> 01:10:10.160
If you want to argue that defund

01:10:10.160 --> 01:10:12.780
is a bad electoral message, great.

01:10:12.780 --> 01:10:15.620
Tell politicians they
shouldn't say defund the police

01:10:15.620 --> 01:10:18.870
which almost no politician
did by the way running

01:10:18.870 --> 01:10:22.150
for national office,
but don't tell activists

01:10:22.150 --> 01:10:23.670
that they should reframe the,

01:10:23.670 --> 01:10:25.457
'cause they don't work for you.

01:10:31.952 --> 01:10:34.860
I'd swear my earbud was fully
charged when we got started.

01:10:34.860 --> 01:10:36.440
Anyway, the activists don't work

01:10:36.440 --> 01:10:38.100
for the Democratic establishment.

01:10:38.100 --> 01:10:39.930
And if the Democratic
establishment would like activist

01:10:39.930 --> 01:10:42.250
to work for them, they
should give them money

01:10:42.250 --> 01:10:44.790
and give them voice in
the democratic platform,

01:10:44.790 --> 01:10:47.710
and until that happens,
they should shut the heck up

01:10:47.710 --> 01:10:49.710
about what activists should be saying

01:10:49.710 --> 01:10:52.040
that's obviously getting
activists, what activists

01:10:52.040 --> 01:10:53.290
are trying to do.

01:10:53.290 --> 01:10:54.800
Folks not doing the work

01:10:54.800 --> 01:10:56.300
should not be telling folks doing the work

01:10:56.300 --> 01:10:57.530
how to do the work.

01:10:57.530 --> 01:11:00.140
It's not just defensive, it's oppressive.

01:11:00.140 --> 01:11:03.870
- Yeah, you know, yeah,
thank you for that response.

01:11:03.870 --> 01:11:05.880
And, you know, one of the things

01:11:05.880 --> 01:11:08.480
that you sort of mentioned
sort of as you started

01:11:08.480 --> 01:11:11.100
the response was that, you know,
there's you as a professor,

01:11:11.100 --> 01:11:12.700
you as a person and there's you

01:11:12.700 --> 01:11:15.970
as CEO of Center of Policing Equity,

01:11:15.970 --> 01:11:17.810
and you know, some of the things

01:11:17.810 --> 01:11:19.170
especially the more
transformative things that you,

01:11:19.170 --> 01:11:23.650
you know, you've shared on
this call in this conversation,

01:11:23.650 --> 01:11:27.560
how much of that gets translated to CPE

01:11:27.560 --> 01:11:30.820
and sort of like what's the
interplay between the two?

01:11:30.820 --> 01:11:31.653
- Yeah, it's a good question

01:11:31.653 --> 01:11:32.486
'cause like at the beginning CPE

01:11:32.486 --> 01:11:34.600
was just my research lab, right,

01:11:34.600 --> 01:11:35.850
like I didn't call it nothing,

01:11:35.850 --> 01:11:37.520
I just had a bunch of
undergraduates running

01:11:37.520 --> 01:11:39.010
around recoding police data

01:11:39.010 --> 01:11:41.513
that nobody had ever had
access to before, right.

01:11:42.600 --> 01:11:45.450
So, it became a larger university thing

01:11:45.450 --> 01:11:49.790
when I moved from State College
at Penn State out to UCLA

01:11:49.790 --> 01:11:50.623
and then it was called

01:11:50.623 --> 01:11:52.480
the Consortium for Police
Leadership in Equity

01:11:52.480 --> 01:11:54.233
'cause I was bad at naming things.

01:11:55.260 --> 01:11:59.340
But it wasn't even a
non-profit until 2017, right.

01:11:59.340 --> 01:12:03.750
At that point it gets a
board and the board says,

01:12:03.750 --> 01:12:04.583
you can take money from here,

01:12:04.583 --> 01:12:06.724
you can't take money from here.

01:12:06.724 --> 01:12:09.610
And at that point it is an
institution outside of me.

01:12:09.610 --> 01:12:12.600
So, I'm a co-founder,
it's me and Tracie Keesee.

01:12:12.600 --> 01:12:15.683
Our ideology, our politics
are pretty similar,

01:12:17.470 --> 01:12:19.513
but the organization has a role to play.

01:12:20.510 --> 01:12:23.540
And this is the thing that
I hope that folks get.

01:12:23.540 --> 01:12:26.700
To get the kind of change
that I think again,

01:12:26.700 --> 01:12:28.800
there's broad ideological commitment to

01:12:29.740 --> 01:12:31.090
that abolitionists have been calling for

01:12:31.090 --> 01:12:32.750
and that folks who aren't abolitionists

01:12:32.750 --> 01:12:34.230
but have played abolitionists
in their spare time

01:12:34.230 --> 01:12:37.010
but they call it for a long time,

01:12:37.010 --> 01:12:38.480
to get that kind of change I don't think

01:12:38.480 --> 01:12:41.660
we get there without an outside
game and an inside game.

01:12:41.660 --> 01:12:43.080
I think there's going to be setbacks.

01:12:43.080 --> 01:12:45.020
There's gonna be failures
that are really public.

01:12:45.020 --> 01:12:47.320
I was really concerned
after that (mumbles) piece

01:12:47.320 --> 01:12:48.790
I got published in The Times

01:12:48.790 --> 01:12:51.900
and there was the podcast
around it about Minneapolis

01:12:51.900 --> 01:12:54.823
'cause it framed it in
the worst possible way.

01:12:55.730 --> 01:12:58.260
And so, the lane that
CPE is likely to stay in

01:12:58.260 --> 01:13:03.240
for some time is going
to be straddling outside

01:13:03.240 --> 01:13:04.203
and inside game.

01:13:05.040 --> 01:13:08.300
I don't think you're
likely to see abolition

01:13:08.300 --> 01:13:11.930
as the mission of CPE on its
website though, to be fair

01:13:11.930 --> 01:13:13.320
and I know, it's going on YouTube

01:13:13.320 --> 01:13:15.320
so I won't talk too bad
about CPE's website.

01:13:15.320 --> 01:13:17.120
The website is gonna need some help.

01:13:18.679 --> 01:13:19.970
It's in the works.

01:13:19.970 --> 01:13:21.102
But I still don't think

01:13:21.102 --> 01:13:21.949
you're gonna see it on CPE's website

01:13:21.949 --> 01:13:25.523
'cause that's not the lane that CPE is in.

01:13:27.760 --> 01:13:31.070
But I worry that we're in this moment

01:13:31.070 --> 01:13:34.790
of real short-sightedness
in terms of the history

01:13:34.790 --> 01:13:35.623
of this struggle.

01:13:35.623 --> 01:13:36.850
I really appreciate that you put this

01:13:36.850 --> 01:13:39.450
in the context of a long
struggle for black freedom,

01:13:40.840 --> 01:13:42.490
and that we're throwing away...

01:13:42.490 --> 01:13:47.490
I see stuff in meetings from
funders, from allies in this.

01:13:48.560 --> 01:13:51.310
Well, we got white folks talking
about their abolitionists

01:13:51.310 --> 01:13:53.990
and referring to black
women on this college campus

01:13:53.990 --> 01:13:57.563
as agile model, getting
retweeted and getting lifted up.

01:13:58.490 --> 01:13:59.713
I don't understand it.

01:14:00.590 --> 01:14:04.580
There is a kind of venal toxicity

01:14:04.580 --> 01:14:08.020
in portions of this space that are,

01:14:08.020 --> 01:14:10.430
it's just counter to the goal.

01:14:10.430 --> 01:14:12.780
So, CPE will likely to
continue to play a role

01:14:12.780 --> 01:14:14.750
where it straddles the
inside, outside game.

01:14:14.750 --> 01:14:16.670
We have had very little outside game

01:14:16.670 --> 01:14:19.140
prior to nine months ago that was public.

01:14:19.140 --> 01:14:21.420
I'm proud of what we've done previously

01:14:21.420 --> 01:14:23.540
but it was less than 20% of what we did.

01:14:23.540 --> 01:14:26.160
It's more than 50% of what we're planning

01:14:26.160 --> 01:14:28.580
and likely even larger than that

01:14:28.580 --> 01:14:30.270
in the next five to 10 years assuming

01:14:30.270 --> 01:14:32.383
that it gets to exist
for five to 10 years.

01:14:33.600 --> 01:14:37.840
But just because the tactics
and the lane are different

01:14:37.840 --> 01:14:40.330
doesn't mean that they
have to be misaligned

01:14:41.510 --> 01:14:43.090
and that's what I'm hoping we get to.

01:14:43.090 --> 01:14:45.990
I hope we're talking more
about the appropriate responses

01:14:45.990 --> 01:14:49.240
to violence that don't require punishment

01:14:49.240 --> 01:14:50.390
that could require investment,

01:14:50.390 --> 01:14:53.500
that we see violence as a
policy failure every time,

01:14:53.500 --> 01:14:54.910
and that we're able to build coalition

01:14:54.910 --> 01:14:59.900
across the various lanes 'cause
if we're making bold demands

01:14:59.900 --> 01:15:03.363
and we got no body bringing
the middle along with us,

01:15:04.360 --> 01:15:07.617
it's easy for that to just
break off and we get nothing.

01:15:07.617 --> 01:15:09.590
And the one thing that makes me most tired

01:15:09.590 --> 01:15:12.110
at all this is the number
of times that black folks

01:15:12.110 --> 01:15:15.280
have gotten organized,
articulated very clearly

01:15:15.280 --> 01:15:17.350
the ways in which this country owes

01:15:17.350 --> 01:15:21.980
for our sacrifice, our
labor and we get nothing.

01:15:21.980 --> 01:15:23.130
I don't, I'm tired of seeing that.

01:15:23.130 --> 01:15:24.820
I don't wanna see that in this moment

01:15:24.820 --> 01:15:26.000
and I don't wanna build things

01:15:26.000 --> 01:15:27.600
that are likely to produce that.

01:15:29.370 --> 01:15:34.020
- Yeah, we have a question
bringing it home to Yale.

01:15:34.020 --> 01:15:37.020
I know that this is your,
you know, your first year

01:15:37.020 --> 01:15:39.040
but, you know, I'm sure you do know

01:15:39.040 --> 01:15:40.440
that there's been a campaign

01:15:40.440 --> 01:15:42.890
on Black Students for Disarmament at Yale

01:15:42.890 --> 01:15:45.190
and they've launched an
invest divest campaign

01:15:45.190 --> 01:15:49.480
and yeah, and so, you know,
I'm wondering your thoughts,

01:15:49.480 --> 01:15:52.560
you know, do you support
that sort of that work

01:15:52.560 --> 01:15:55.910
and yeah, you know, a lot

01:15:55.910 --> 01:15:57.840
of these conversations involve
different forms of policing

01:15:57.840 --> 01:16:00.370
but the conversation's around other kinds

01:16:00.370 --> 01:16:02.470
of besides municipal policing
like college campus police

01:16:02.470 --> 01:16:04.909
as well as sheriffs, state troopers,

01:16:04.909 --> 01:16:07.490
you know, federal police as we see sort of

01:16:07.490 --> 01:16:09.100
in the Trump administration,

01:16:09.100 --> 01:16:11.180
but thinking about college campuses

01:16:11.180 --> 01:16:13.920
and yeah, what that looks like,

01:16:13.920 --> 01:16:16.110
especially because, you
know, an organization

01:16:16.110 --> 01:16:18.510
like CPE saying we support something

01:16:18.510 --> 01:16:20.540
like invest divest at a
college campus, like yeah,

01:16:20.540 --> 01:16:23.100
would carry like weight,
and so I'm just, yeah,

01:16:23.100 --> 01:16:25.600
I'm wondering your thoughts
and how you relate to that all.

01:16:25.600 --> 01:16:28.080
- Yeah, and so I got
to say, I am frustrated

01:16:28.080 --> 01:16:31.930
at the scheduling of meetings
of (mumbles) section.

01:16:31.930 --> 01:16:34.740
I've been asked to a couple
and I've had to not be able.

01:16:34.740 --> 01:16:37.550
I just, the conflict was unavoidable.

01:16:37.550 --> 01:16:38.860
So, I can't say that I know all

01:16:38.860 --> 01:16:40.550
of the specifics of the tactics.

01:16:40.550 --> 01:16:43.000
I will say I've been involved
in some conversations

01:16:43.000 --> 01:16:45.080
across the campus about what it looks like

01:16:45.080 --> 01:16:48.830
to abolish New Haven police
or what I hear more frequently

01:16:48.830 --> 01:16:52.130
what it looks like to abolish
the Yale police department.

01:16:52.130 --> 01:16:55.800
I am in favor of all
invest in black communities

01:16:55.800 --> 01:16:57.000
and vulnerable communities

01:16:57.000 --> 01:16:59.720
and take that money from punishment.

01:16:59.720 --> 01:17:01.930
I am personally very much in favor of that

01:17:01.930 --> 01:17:03.623
in every place that we can do it.

01:17:04.470 --> 01:17:06.440
I think in the context of a university

01:17:06.440 --> 01:17:08.290
it could be really interesting

01:17:08.290 --> 01:17:13.290
'cause the real sort of contrary
audience here are parents,

01:17:15.500 --> 01:17:20.040
and if you can get college
parents to get behind this,

01:17:20.040 --> 01:17:24.140
then there's a lot of
models for what can happen

01:17:24.140 --> 01:17:27.300
across college campuses, right?

01:17:27.300 --> 01:17:28.940
Where it gets complicated to me

01:17:28.940 --> 01:17:31.450
in terms of the local politics of it,

01:17:31.450 --> 01:17:34.643
you take Yale PD and
you throw it out, okay.

01:17:35.950 --> 01:17:37.370
As of right now I can tell you,

01:17:37.370 --> 01:17:39.190
and I've now had conversations

01:17:39.190 --> 01:17:41.520
with probably a dozen colleges

01:17:41.520 --> 01:17:45.020
across the country, parents are saying,

01:17:45.020 --> 01:17:47.850
that's all fine and good
but there better be cops

01:17:47.850 --> 01:17:50.690
when my baby has a
complaint to make, right.

01:17:50.690 --> 01:17:52.200
My baby better be safe

01:17:52.200 --> 01:17:54.860
and I'm not re-imagining
nothing, right, I imagine

01:17:54.860 --> 01:17:56.870
that if there's not someone
to call, I'mma sue you.

01:17:56.870 --> 01:17:58.290
I'm gonna take all your money,

01:17:58.290 --> 01:17:59.450
like your endowment's gonna be smaller

01:17:59.450 --> 01:18:01.477
by the time I'm done with you, right.

01:18:01.477 --> 01:18:04.920
And so what would happen, if
it's New Haven law enforcement

01:18:04.920 --> 01:18:06.690
that comes in as supposed
to Yale law enforcement

01:18:06.690 --> 01:18:08.630
that comes in, right.

01:18:08.630 --> 01:18:11.640
When I go to Eastern parts and Fair Haven,

01:18:11.640 --> 01:18:14.790
so I'm not living in New
Haven yet, I'm looking.

01:18:14.790 --> 01:18:15.793
When I go to black parts of New Haven

01:18:15.793 --> 01:18:20.793
what I hear in my quick
ethnographies, I'm seeing

01:18:21.210 --> 01:18:22.890
we have somebody here who
spent time at barbershops.

01:18:22.890 --> 01:18:26.380
Maurice is in here, right, when
I'm looking for barbershops,

01:18:26.380 --> 01:18:27.350
what I hear is folks saying,

01:18:27.350 --> 01:18:28.890
they noticed the demonstrating
in Yale at New Haven.

01:18:28.890 --> 01:18:31.640
They'd rather get stopped by
Yale PD, the New Haven PD.

01:18:32.860 --> 01:18:36.550
And so, is the goal a
reduction in New Haven

01:18:36.550 --> 01:18:41.430
or is the goal a destruction
of Yale PD and an expansion...

01:18:41.430 --> 01:18:42.963
how does it get done?

01:18:44.020 --> 01:18:46.553
And so, what I'm interested
in and what I'm hoping

01:18:46.553 --> 01:18:48.230
will be part of the conversation

01:18:48.230 --> 01:18:50.080
when I get to engage with the undergrads

01:18:50.080 --> 01:18:52.270
as well as the graduate
group and increasingly

01:18:52.270 --> 01:18:56.200
there's a faculty group
that's interested in this,

01:18:56.200 --> 01:18:57.433
what is the invest part?

01:18:58.920 --> 01:19:02.610
What gets stood up, at what
pace with what investment

01:19:02.610 --> 01:19:05.190
and how do we then
message the ways in which

01:19:05.190 --> 01:19:10.080
that can allow us to divest
from structures of punishment?

01:19:10.080 --> 01:19:12.680
University of Pennsylvania
just committed a $100 million

01:19:12.680 --> 01:19:15.130
to Philadelphia public schools, right?

01:19:15.130 --> 01:19:16.930
That is a great PR campaign.

01:19:16.930 --> 01:19:18.030
I'm really interested to see how

01:19:18.030 --> 01:19:20.870
that money gets actually invested, right?

01:19:20.870 --> 01:19:23.340
But if U Penn can do something like that

01:19:23.340 --> 01:19:25.710
what is Yale prepared to do

01:19:25.710 --> 01:19:29.130
to make sure that particularly
black residents of New Haven

01:19:29.130 --> 01:19:31.130
are positioned to be safer

01:19:31.130 --> 01:19:33.270
without the need for law enforcement?

01:19:33.270 --> 01:19:34.103
That's the part that I'd...

01:19:34.103 --> 01:19:39.103
So, I'm generally endorsed
and favorable to the position.

01:19:39.380 --> 01:19:41.750
I would very much like
to sit down and talk

01:19:41.750 --> 01:19:42.800
and to the degree

01:19:42.800 --> 01:19:44.650
that I can be useful
advocating, hell yeah.

01:19:44.650 --> 01:19:48.100
All of my conversations so
far, every university has been,

01:19:48.100 --> 01:19:50.640
how do you put university
police out of business?

01:19:50.640 --> 01:19:52.350
And if that is not your goal

01:19:52.350 --> 01:19:54.523
then I would suggest you
get a different goal.

01:19:55.890 --> 01:19:57.390
- We have a question from Damarlos

01:19:57.390 --> 01:20:00.360
which is similar to some of the things

01:20:00.360 --> 01:20:02.150
you were just mentioning

01:20:02.150 --> 01:20:03.980
and that I brought up in the question.

01:20:03.980 --> 01:20:06.320
He says violence is not the
only community safety concern

01:20:06.320 --> 01:20:07.360
facing black communities.

01:20:07.360 --> 01:20:08.970
Cities and government officials often talk

01:20:08.970 --> 01:20:11.870
about addressing social
inequalities in the abstract,

01:20:11.870 --> 01:20:13.120
but are unwilling to actually change

01:20:13.120 --> 01:20:15.070
their investment strategies
or policy priorities

01:20:15.070 --> 01:20:17.190
to realize those changes.

01:20:17.190 --> 01:20:19.210
From your perspective, how
do we shift conversations

01:20:19.210 --> 01:20:21.760
about community from solely
focusing on eradicating violence

01:20:21.760 --> 01:20:25.290
to focus on sustaining
social determinants of health

01:20:25.290 --> 01:20:27.230
and eradicating persistent
social inequalities?

01:20:27.230 --> 01:20:31.010
Is CPE actively working to
shift the narrative in this way?

01:20:31.010 --> 01:20:33.800
- Yeah, and so I wanna do a both end here.

01:20:33.800 --> 01:20:37.123
Thank you Damara as
always for coming correct.

01:20:38.390 --> 01:20:40.960
I want us to talk about violence

01:20:40.960 --> 01:20:44.240
because I want us to understand
if we let out everybody

01:20:44.240 --> 01:20:48.290
who is incarcerated right now
or was on state monitoring,

01:20:48.290 --> 01:20:50.110
gets non-violent, we got what?

01:20:50.110 --> 01:20:52.593
3/4 of folks still left locked up.

01:20:53.780 --> 01:20:56.270
I want the big changes and
if we don't reckon with,

01:20:56.270 --> 01:20:59.240
I mean, it is hard now
not to be responsive

01:20:59.240 --> 01:21:01.330
to Danielle Sered's book,
right, "Until We Reckon."

01:21:01.330 --> 01:21:03.420
But we don't reckon with violence,

01:21:03.420 --> 01:21:05.320
I worry we're not gonna
have the tools that we need

01:21:05.320 --> 01:21:06.330
and at the same time violence

01:21:06.330 --> 01:21:10.030
is not the only safety
concern, absolutely.

01:21:10.030 --> 01:21:12.880
I am less interested
in decentering violence

01:21:12.880 --> 01:21:15.530
than I am in expanding the center

01:21:15.530 --> 01:21:16.610
to talk about what communities

01:21:16.610 --> 01:21:18.330
are interested in talking about.

01:21:18.330 --> 01:21:22.020
But I'll say in addition that when I go

01:21:22.020 --> 01:21:23.690
and I'm listening to community meetings,

01:21:23.690 --> 01:21:26.440
when I was walking around in New Haven

01:21:26.440 --> 01:21:28.520
and talking to folks I
was like, hey, so what

01:21:28.520 --> 01:21:29.410
does it mean to be safe here?

01:21:29.410 --> 01:21:32.670
I had real interesting
conversation as my wife and I

01:21:32.670 --> 01:21:33.660
were house on it, probably

01:21:33.660 --> 01:21:35.593
what does community safety here mean?

01:21:37.880 --> 01:21:40.150
It ends up being either deeply abstracted.

01:21:40.150 --> 01:21:43.310
I mean, the conversation
was just leave us alone.

01:21:43.310 --> 01:21:45.630
Just let us be.

01:21:45.630 --> 01:21:49.370
I wanna walk around and know
that it's nobody's right

01:21:49.370 --> 01:21:50.203
to stop me.

01:21:50.203 --> 01:21:51.200
And I was like, oh, I'm sorry.

01:21:51.200 --> 01:21:53.500
I don't mean, he's like, no,
no, I'm good talking to you.

01:21:53.500 --> 01:21:55.400
I just like, I know I could walk away.

01:21:56.760 --> 01:21:58.820
That's kind of abstract.

01:21:58.820 --> 01:22:01.650
The other things that I hear
end up being low level quality

01:22:01.650 --> 01:22:06.030
of life things and it'd
be great to focus on...

01:22:06.030 --> 01:22:09.520
That's, I don't want that in
the public safety conversation,

01:22:09.520 --> 01:22:10.980
the quality of life stuff I want

01:22:10.980 --> 01:22:13.230
in the public health conversation.

01:22:13.230 --> 01:22:15.110
So, the way it's, we just
had a meeting yesterday with,

01:22:15.110 --> 01:22:15.943
I don't know if you guys

01:22:15.943 --> 01:22:17.240
have been following American
Medical Association,

01:22:17.240 --> 01:22:19.040
just put out a list of principles

01:22:19.040 --> 01:22:23.520
they call white supremacy
and police brutality

01:22:23.520 --> 01:22:25.980
as white supremacy a
public health problem.

01:22:25.980 --> 01:22:26.813
We've been working with them

01:22:26.813 --> 01:22:28.230
for a minute trying to get that out.

01:22:28.230 --> 01:22:29.720
I'm so excited and proud

01:22:29.720 --> 01:22:31.250
that the American Medical Association

01:22:31.250 --> 01:22:34.200
has a statement on police
brutality as public health issues.

01:22:34.200 --> 01:22:37.110
We've been working with
Robert Wood Johnson

01:22:37.110 --> 01:22:38.140
and other funders

01:22:38.140 --> 01:22:41.060
to make public safety
a public health issue,

01:22:41.060 --> 01:22:43.720
and to take public health,
allow that to segment out

01:22:43.720 --> 01:22:44.930
the things that police
shouldn't have been dealing with

01:22:44.930 --> 01:22:46.510
in the first place.

01:22:46.510 --> 01:22:47.890
So, that's behind the scene stuff.

01:22:47.890 --> 01:22:50.100
It's not part of the public portfolio.

01:22:50.100 --> 01:22:51.660
I think we'll be messaging about that.

01:22:51.660 --> 01:22:54.880
We now have a brand new director
of public health at CPE.

01:22:54.880 --> 01:22:56.750
We'll be messaging about that.

01:22:56.750 --> 01:22:58.870
But I think the longest route,

01:22:58.870 --> 01:23:01.720
if I can make my answer
a little bit simpler,

01:23:01.720 --> 01:23:03.547
the longest shot is I
want to center violence

01:23:03.547 --> 01:23:06.040
and public safety 'cause I
think that's a conversation

01:23:06.040 --> 01:23:08.600
we need to be having
and way more concretely.

01:23:08.600 --> 01:23:09.950
And I wanna take most of the other things

01:23:09.950 --> 01:23:11.740
that kind of are public safety

01:23:11.740 --> 01:23:13.290
and remove them from that

01:23:13.290 --> 01:23:14.520
and put them in public health

01:23:14.520 --> 01:23:15.570
'cause a public health response

01:23:15.570 --> 01:23:18.060
will be radically different
than a punitive one.

01:23:18.060 --> 01:23:19.240
Does that make sense?

01:23:19.240 --> 01:23:20.590
- Yeah.

01:23:20.590 --> 01:23:21.950
Yeah, thank you for that response.

01:23:21.950 --> 01:23:26.710
And so, I wanted sort of, and
we're nearing towards the end,

01:23:26.710 --> 01:23:28.910
but one of the things that
I did wanna ask you about

01:23:28.910 --> 01:23:31.010
is sort of, you know, as you shared

01:23:31.010 --> 01:23:32.270
with the previous question

01:23:32.270 --> 01:23:35.563
that you sort of imagined
abolitionists horizon,

01:23:36.790 --> 01:23:40.730
but I know in a CNN article
from a few months ago,

01:23:40.730 --> 01:23:45.131
you seemingly argued for limited
but viable role for police.

01:23:45.131 --> 01:23:47.410
And, you know, you said
that suburbs are an example

01:23:48.340 --> 01:23:49.560
of what that can look like.

01:23:49.560 --> 01:23:52.430
And so, I'm wondering,
because those things seem,

01:23:52.430 --> 01:23:53.750
you know, a bit different, I'm wondering

01:23:53.750 --> 01:23:57.760
if you can talk more about
that sort of, and if, you know,

01:23:57.760 --> 01:24:00.680
in that model sort of
what you imagine police

01:24:00.680 --> 01:24:02.240
should be involved within that context

01:24:02.240 --> 01:24:04.020
and sort of I'm hearing
a bit of a disjunction,

01:24:04.020 --> 01:24:05.980
so I'm just wondering about that.

01:24:05.980 --> 01:24:08.450
And also, you know,
suburbs are also, you know,

01:24:08.450 --> 01:24:09.880
they seemingly seem like,

01:24:09.880 --> 01:24:11.430
you know, sites of not police officers,

01:24:11.430 --> 01:24:12.600
but when you go and, you know,

01:24:12.600 --> 01:24:14.900
there's often black
models in those contexts

01:24:16.419 --> 01:24:18.260
that do experience routine racial,

01:24:18.260 --> 01:24:21.090
you know, racialized police
violence discrimination.

01:24:21.090 --> 01:24:23.310
But yeah, I'm just interested
in hearing more about that.

01:24:23.310 --> 01:24:25.700
- Yeah, so we published that in the midst

01:24:25.700 --> 01:24:28.510
of sort of the ground shifting under feet.

01:24:28.510 --> 01:24:31.410
And so what I was trying
to do in that article

01:24:31.410 --> 01:24:35.810
was to make the idea of reducing
the size of law enforcement

01:24:35.810 --> 01:24:39.250
and increasing community
control less exotic

01:24:39.250 --> 01:24:42.070
for the hundreds of
mayors and city councils

01:24:42.070 --> 01:24:43.070
who were reaching out to us,

01:24:43.070 --> 01:24:44.840
like this does not have to be scary.

01:24:44.840 --> 01:24:46.950
And I can't do that by making reference

01:24:46.950 --> 01:24:50.570
to Afro-futurism and the
long history of abolitionism

01:24:50.570 --> 01:24:53.020
within black intellectual tradition

01:24:53.020 --> 01:24:54.800
'cause they're not trying
to hear any of that.

01:24:54.800 --> 01:24:58.780
So, the talking point was,
have you been to a suburb?

01:24:58.780 --> 01:25:01.380
The footprint of law
enforcement is less per capita.

01:25:02.230 --> 01:25:04.040
If anybody in the community or any group

01:25:04.040 --> 01:25:06.450
within the community is outspoken enough,

01:25:06.450 --> 01:25:08.390
it changes tactics, right?

01:25:08.390 --> 01:25:11.717
It is much more Mayberry than
what we get in urban policing.

01:25:11.717 --> 01:25:13.470
And that was mostly for the urban folks

01:25:13.470 --> 01:25:15.040
who were being responsive to that.

01:25:15.040 --> 01:25:15.920
Now, I don't know whether or not

01:25:15.920 --> 01:25:17.090
it made it into the final draft,

01:25:17.090 --> 01:25:18.600
but I did have a section saying

01:25:18.600 --> 01:25:20.870
that is, of course, only if you live there

01:25:20.870 --> 01:25:22.750
'cause if you're passing through

01:25:22.750 --> 01:25:24.940
those are terrible sites of violence.

01:25:24.940 --> 01:25:27.260
And what we know is that in
smaller police department,

01:25:27.260 --> 01:25:30.900
the smaller it gets the higher
the rate of deadly force.

01:25:30.900 --> 01:25:31.810
In part they'll argue

01:25:31.810 --> 01:25:34.480
because of lack of
training but regardless.

01:25:34.480 --> 01:25:39.010
So, the idea is to start making
a new world more familiar

01:25:39.010 --> 01:25:41.823
to folks who've got the ability
to make those decisions.

01:25:42.930 --> 01:25:44.160
I still think that that makes sense

01:25:44.160 --> 01:25:46.670
as a talking point to
a particular audience.

01:25:46.670 --> 01:25:49.040
I don't use it in most
of the conversations

01:25:49.040 --> 01:25:51.700
that I'm having right now
for exactly that reason,

01:25:51.700 --> 01:25:54.010
'cause we think of suburban
as a cookie cutter.

01:25:54.010 --> 01:25:55.380
We think about it as only white

01:25:55.380 --> 01:25:57.790
but black people live
in the suburbs, right?

01:25:57.790 --> 01:25:59.280
There's great diversity within suburbs

01:25:59.280 --> 01:26:02.190
especially depending on how
they're set up and drawn,

01:26:02.190 --> 01:26:04.560
I mean, so within suburbs you
have massive stratification

01:26:04.560 --> 01:26:06.840
in the same way that
you do in urban areas,

01:26:06.840 --> 01:26:11.620
it just looks different in
terms of the experience of it.

01:26:11.620 --> 01:26:12.790
But that was just the goal

01:26:12.790 --> 01:26:15.990
is to make a future state less exotic

01:26:15.990 --> 01:26:19.200
for the people who were trying
to make moves in August.

01:26:19.200 --> 01:26:21.240
I think that's when they came out, August.

01:26:21.240 --> 01:26:22.770
- Yeah, August September.

01:26:22.770 --> 01:26:24.960
Yeah, I mean, I think we're at about time

01:26:24.960 --> 01:26:28.040
and, you know, again I thank
everyone for coming out today

01:26:28.040 --> 01:26:30.253
and engaging in this conversation with us,

01:26:31.230 --> 01:26:33.400
you know, and I hope if
nothing else it's brought,

01:26:33.400 --> 01:26:36.000
you know, a sense of
clarity around, you know,

01:26:36.000 --> 01:26:38.200
the conversations around
race and policing.

01:26:38.200 --> 01:26:40.100
I mean importantly, you
know, I think there's,

01:26:40.100 --> 01:26:42.807
you know, to acknowledge
that there can be,

01:26:42.807 --> 01:26:46.010
and I think clarity envisions

01:26:46.010 --> 01:26:47.630
can be formed through struggle,

01:26:47.630 --> 01:26:50.840
and, you know, I think there
can be sometimes an attempt to,

01:26:50.840 --> 01:26:53.030
you know, push and try to
say, you know, everyone

01:26:53.030 --> 01:26:54.560
is saying the same thing
and things like that

01:26:54.560 --> 01:26:56.930
but I think it's important
to hold, you know,

01:26:56.930 --> 01:26:58.340
when people are saying different things

01:26:58.340 --> 01:27:01.760
and hold the complexity and
have, you know, conversations.

01:27:01.760 --> 01:27:04.170
And I hope that we can continue, you know,

01:27:04.170 --> 01:27:07.663
these conversations and yeah,
thank you everyone for coming.

